WEBVTT

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:15.320
<v Ira Glass>David, so, what problem was this supposed to solve?
<v Ira Glass>大卫，所以，这原本是为了解决什么问题？

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.510
<v David Kestenbaum>The teachers were trying to get the kids to stop tattling.
<v David Kestenbaum>老师们试图让孩子们停止打小报告。

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.720
<v Ira Glass>And why was that so important?
<v Ira Glass>为什么那件事那么重要？

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.170
<v David Kestenbaum>Because it's a pre-K classroom, and it's constant there.
<v David Kestenbaum>因为那是学前班的教室，在那儿这种情况层出不穷。

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:33.780
<v Ira Glass>Right. I should say, by the way, that you are David Kestenbaum. You are one of our producers, our managing editor right now. And this classroom is your kid's classroom, right?
<v Ira Glass>对。顺便说一下，我得介绍一下，你是大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆。你是我们的制片人之一，也是我们现在的总编辑。而这个教室是你孩子的教室，对吧？

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.100
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. Both of them went there.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。他们两个都在那儿上过。

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.660
<v Ira Glass>I still don't get-- it seems like kids tattling on each other is just a fact of pre-K.
<v Ira Glass>我还是不明白——孩子们互相打小报告似乎是学前班的常态。

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:52.760
<v David Kestenbaum>It is, but you've got 20-something kids. I mean, you know, it's hard being one parent with two kids doing it. Imagine you're in a classroom with, like, 20 kids. And if they bring it to you as a teacher, all these little injustices, you've got to get involved in all these cases, you know?
<v David Kestenbaum>确实是，但你有二十多个孩子。我的意思是，你知道，身为一个有两个孩子的家长处理这些事都很累。想象一下你在一个有20个孩子的教室里。如果他们作为老师把所有这些小小的纠纷都带给你，你得介入所有这些案件，你懂的？

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.030
<v Ira Glass>Right. And it's just a time suck.
<v Ira Glass>对。这纯粹是在浪费时间。

00:00:54.030 --> 00:01:15.650
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, it's just a time suck. And so one of the teachers had this great idea. She took a tissue box, hung it on the wall, and then took this plastic phone receiver and hung it in it and said something like, that's the tattle-phone. Tell it to the phone. And I thought it was just brilliant.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，就是在浪费时间。所以其中一位老师想到了一个绝妙的主意。她拿了一个纸巾盒，挂在墙上，然后在里面挂了一个塑料电话听筒，并说类似“那是告密电话（tattle-phone）。对着电话说吧”。我觉得这简直太天才了。

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:28.350
<v David Kestenbaum>And I just want to say, this is a place that loves kids. I would go to pick up my son Max, and I would have to wait because he would want to hug every single teacher. And I would go to get Auggie, our older kid, and he would be like, I need five minutes, I'm in the middle of something. Like--
<v David Kestenbaum>我只想说，这是一个充满爱心的地方。我去接我儿子马克斯时，还得等一会儿，因为他想拥抱每一位老师。我去接我们的大儿子奥吉时，他会说，我需要五分钟，我正忙着呢。就像——

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.000
<v Ira Glass>They just want to stay longer.
<v Ira Glass>他们只是想多待一会儿。

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:42.870
<v David Kestenbaum>I mean, I get choked up thinking about this place. And I love the idea of the tattle-phone, just as a vessel for all the little injustices that go on every day. And I wanted to hear what was said into it-- like, these little three year olds, four year olds talking into this plastic phone.
<v David Kestenbaum>我的意思是，想到这个地方我就哽咽。我很喜欢这个告密电话的创意，它就像每天发生的所有小纠纷的容器。我想听听人们对着它说了什么——比如这些三四岁的小家伙对着这个塑料电话说了些什么。

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.440
<v Ira Glass>OK. And I should say, this is the part of the story I know very well what happens next.
<v Ira Glass>好的。我得说，这是故事中我知道接下来会发生什么的那一部分。

00:01:46.440 --> 00:02:04.230
<v David Kestenbaum>We asked if we could put a phone in the classroom that would record the tattles. The daycare was fine with that. I reached out to the parents. I didn't know how they were going to feel about us recording their kids at, like, the worst moment of the day. But every single one of them said yes. Some of them said please, yes, do this. So we got a phone.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们询问是否可以在教室里放一部电话来录下这些告密信息。日托中心对此没有意见。我联系了家长们。我不知道他们对于我们在他们孩子一天中最糟糕的时刻录音会有什么感觉。但他们每个人都答应了。有些人甚至说，拜托，是的，一定要这么做。所以我们拿到了电话。

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:11.910
<v David Kestenbaum>It was this chunky red phone, kind of like 1980s style, we had it set up to record. And we needed an outgoing message for it.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是一部粗笨的红色电话，有点像80年代风格的，我们把它设置成录音模式。我们需要一条外呼提示音。

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.470
<v Ira Glass>And that is where I stepped in.
<v Ira Glass>而那就是我介入的地方。

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:21.450
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, you've reached the tattle phone. OK, tell me what happened after the beep. Tell me the whole story.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，你打通了告密电话。好的，请在哔声后告诉我发生了什么。把整个故事告诉我。

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:37.040
<v Ira Glass>I really hope to make some inroads in that four-year-old market. I really hope that helps our ratings. So what we're going to do now, David, is that you're going to explain what you heard on those recordings on the tattle-phone. And when you're done, I'm going to come back, and I'll explain what today's show is about.
<v Ira Glass>我真的希望能在这个四岁儿童市场打开局面。我真的希望这能提高我们的收视率。那么大卫，我们现在要做的是，你来解释一下你在告密电话的录音里听到了什么。等你讲完，我会回来解释今天节目讲述的内容。

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:44.110
<v David Kestenbaum>OK. So I brought the phone into class, and I set it up. And the kids started to use it immediately and with great enthusiasm.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的。我把电话带进了教室，并把它架好了。孩子们立刻就开始使用它，而且非常热心。

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:48.250
<v Kid 1>Eli told me a lie.
<v Kid 1>伊莱对我说了谎。

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:55.000
<v Kid 2>Seamus wasn't sharing with me, and I don't like it, and I'm very upset.
<v Kid 2>谢默斯不跟我分享，我不喜欢这样，我很难过。

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.960
<v Kid 3>Nathan farted in my face, and I said, yuck, Nathan.
<v Kid 3>内森冲着我的脸放屁，我说，真恶心，内森。

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.550
<v David Kestenbaum>Catch that one? Nathan farted in my face, and I said, yuck, Nathan. But the real crime?
<v David Kestenbaum>听见那个了吗？内森冲着我的脸放屁，我说，真恶心，内森。但真正的罪行是？

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.850
<v Kid 3>And he didn't say excuse me.
<v Kid 3>而且他没说“对不起”。

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:29.250
<v David Kestenbaum>I think there was enormous excitement just having a phone in the class. It was larger than they'd probably ever seen in their lives-- bright red with buttons on it. It was an old touch-tone phone. Everyone wanted to use it, even if they didn't have a tattle. Kids sang a song into it. One tried to order a pizza. Someone said, hi mom, please pick me up early.
<v David Kestenbaum>我想教室里有一部电话本身就让他们感到无比兴奋。它比他们一生中见过的任何东西都大——鲜红色，上面有按钮。那是一部老式按键电话。每个人都想用它，即使他们没有小报告要打。孩子们对着它唱歌。有一个孩子试图订披萨。有人说，嘿妈妈，请早点来接我。

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.810
<v Kid 4>Hi, Dad. I love you. Bye.
<v Kid 4>嘿，爸爸。我爱你。再见。

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:45.980
<v Kid 5>Uh, our baby turned into a ducky, and I don't know how it turn into a ducky. And that's why her turned into a ducky and turned back to a baby.
<v Kid 5>呃，我们的宝宝变成了一只小鸭子，我不知道它是怎么变成小鸭子的。这就是为什么它变成了一只小鸭子，然后又变回了宝宝。

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:52.220
<v Kid 6>Uh-- I'm sorry, I need to hang up on you for one second.
<v Kid 6>呃——抱歉，我得挂断你一秒钟。

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.260
<v David Kestenbaum>Several calls later, conscience heavy, that kid calls back.
<v David Kestenbaum>几个电话之后，由于良心不安，那个孩子又打了回来。

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:06.210
<v Kid 6>I'm sorry I had to hang up on you. I'm just sorry.
<v Kid 6>很抱歉我不得不挂断你。我真的很抱歉。

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:23.500
<v David Kestenbaum>The tattle-phone was like a magic portal into one preschool classroom in America, which is kind of incredible. Like most parents I'll ask my kids, what happened in school today, and I get nothing. School is like this black box-- you drop them off, pick them up, and you have no idea what happens in between.
<v David Kestenbaum>告密电话就像通往美国某学前班教室的魔法门户，这真是不可思议。像大多数家长一样，我会问我的孩子，今天学校发生了什么，但我什么都问不到。学校就像一个黑匣子——你送他们去，接他们回来，完全不知道中间发生了什么。

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:51.670
<v David Kestenbaum>But now I had this phone. It was set up so it sent me the messages as they happened. It was funny listening to them from my desk. The portal into this classroom would open for a second, I get this little report from a kid, hear a little sound of the room, and then it would close-- sometimes for a while. Then the portal would open, I'd hear something, and then silence again. There were never any tattles between 1 and 3 o'clock, because that was nap time. Then they'd start again, these little dramas.
<v David Kestenbaum>但现在我有了这个电话。它的设置是让它在消息发生时立即发送给我。在我的办公桌前听这些录音很有趣。通往这个教室的门户会打开一秒钟，我收到某个孩子的小报告，听见一点房间里的声音，然后它就关闭了——有时会关闭很久。然后门户又打开，我听到一些声音，接着又是寂静。下午1点到3点之间从来没有任何告密信息，因为那是午睡时间。然后它们又开始了，这些小小的戏剧。

00:04:51.670 --> 00:05:05.460
<v Kid 7>Ramon is not listening to my teacher, Mr. Evans, but he's my favorite teacher. And I know-- and I know he's mad at me, but I don't want him to be. So I'm trying my best to listen.
<v Kid 7>拉蒙不听我的老师，埃文斯先生的话，但他是我最喜欢的老师。我知道——我知道他在生我的气，但我不想让他生气。所以我正在尽我所能地去听。

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.330
<v Kid 8>My friend Simone said "no" at me.
<v Kid 8>我的朋友西蒙娜对我说“不”。

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.390
<v David Kestenbaum>"My friend Simone." It's always their friends who are bugging them.
<v David Kestenbaum>“我的朋友西蒙娜。”总是他们的朋友在困扰他们。

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:22.020
<v Kid 9>My friend Jack was in my face when I was waiting to go to an area. And that made me really upset.
<v Kid 9>当我在等候区的时候，我的朋友杰克冲到了我的面前。这让我非常难过。

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:28.660
<v Kid 10>(SINGING) Eli hit me. Eli hit me.
<v Kid 10>（唱歌）伊莱打我。伊莱打我。

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:38.680
<v Kid 11>Um, when I'm playing family with Simone, [INAUDIBLE] keeping me awake while I'm trying to sleep, pretend sleep.
<v Kid 11>嗯，当我和西蒙娜玩过家家时，[听不清]在我试图睡觉、假装睡觉的时候让我保持清醒。

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:45.920
<v David Kestenbaum>Can I just say, I'm not sure that one is actionable. You're pretending to sleep and complaining that someone is waking you up? I don't know.
<v David Kestenbaum>我能说一句吗，我不确定那个是可以追究的。你假装睡觉，却抱怨别人把你吵醒？我不知道。

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.090
<v Kid 12>People are not sharing the tattle-phone.
<v Kid 12>大家都没有分享这个告密电话。

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:55.200
<v David Kestenbaum>How much of their day is about, like, justice?
<v David Kestenbaum>他们的一天中有多少比例是关于——比如，正义的？

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:06.480
<v Kathleen Jones>Oh! I couldn't put a percentage on it. But I could say the majority of the day. [LAUGHS]
<v Kathleen Jones>噢！我无法给它定个百分比。但我可以说占了一天中的大部分时间。[笑]

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.710
<v David Kestenbaum>This is Kathleen Jones, the lead teacher in the class.
<v David Kestenbaum>这位是凯瑟琳·琼斯，班里的首席老师。

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:29.940
<v Kathleen Jones>It is everything-- and rules. They live by rules. They can sit down to play a game, and that whole playtime will be nothing but arguing about the rules. And then there's no playtime left, and they feel good about it.
<v Kathleen Jones>那是一切——还有规则。他们靠规则生活。他们可以坐下来玩游戏，而整个游戏时间都将只是在争论规则。然后游戏时间就没了，他们对结果还感到很满意。

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:42.310
<v David Kestenbaum>It's funny. Like, they can't make breakfast for themselves, they can't get dressed, they can barely talk, and they're just full of "that's not fair."
<v David Kestenbaum>这很有趣。就像，他们自己没法做早餐，没法穿衣服，说话都磕磕绊绊，却满口都是“这不公平”。

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:47.080
<v Kathleen Jones>But they're dynamos. Every one of them has something that--
<v Kathleen Jones>但他们是发电机。他们每个人都有某种——

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.530
<v David Kestenbaum>It's awesome.
<v David Kestenbaum>太棒了。

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.370
<v Kathleen Jones>Yes, exactly. It's awesome. They're wonderful.
<v Kathleen Jones>是的，没错。太棒了。他们真是太好了。

00:06:51.370 --> 00:07:12.370
<v David Kestenbaum>There's actual scientific research on this. Kids know when something's unfair, some when they're just 12 months old. My kids weren't even walking then. And I know this is kind of obvious, but so many of the conflicts in this world come down to some version of what's going on in this classroom-- what's fair, how do you divide up something that there's a limited quantity of.
<v David Kestenbaum>这方面有实际的科学研究。孩子在12个月大的时候就知道什么是公平的。那时我的孩子甚至还不会走路。我知道这有点显而易见，但世界上太多的冲突归结为我们这个教室里所发生事情的某种版本——什么是公平的，你如何分配数量有限的东西。

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:30.410
<v Kathleen Jones>You know magnet tiles? We only have one acrylic one that's yellowish gold. That is it. That is the prize in the class. Whoever gets that is like, I'm the king or the queen and ha-ha. The gold-- that's what they call it, the gold.
<v Kathleen Jones>你知道磁力片吗？我们只有一个黄橙色的亚克力片。就这一个。那是班里的奖品。得到它的人就会像说，我是国王或女王，哈哈。那金色的——他们称之为“黄金”。

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:52.520
<v David Kestenbaum>I sometimes think that the main usefulness of numbers for my son Max, the whole reason he knows how to count, is score-keeping-- how many of something he has and how many his brother has. It's a weighing of the scales of justice, no matter how tiny.
<v David Kestenbaum>我有时认为，数字对我儿子马克斯的主要用处，他学习数数的全部原因，就是记分——他有多少东西，他哥哥有多少东西。这是衡量正义天平的一种方式，无论它是多么微小。

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.370
<v David Kestenbaum>So did it work for you? Like, did they stop coming to you with their complaints?
<v David Kestenbaum>那么它对你有用吗？比如，他们停止来找你投诉了吗？

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:03.820
<v Kathleen Jones>Yes, it did. It does work. Yes, that phone holds a lot of power.
<v Kathleen Jones>是的，有效。它确实有效。是的，那部电话掌握着巨大的权力。

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:30.080
<v David Kestenbaum>It gave the teachers the break they were looking for. I wanted to see if the kids thought it worked, whether the tattle-phone helped them deal with stuff. So the other weekend I interviewed a bunch of them-- which, I have to say, these were some of the weirder interviews I've done in my professional life. Bob Woodward, I know this isn't right, but I had to pay one interviewee a piece of gum. Another kid, Nicky, insisted on performing magic tricks before granting an interview. I did not have high expectations.
<v David Kestenbaum>它给了老师们他们想要的休息时间。我想看看孩子们是否觉得它管用，告密电话是否帮他们处理了问题。所以上周末我采访了他们中的一群人——我必须说，这些是我职业生涯中做过的最奇怪的采访之一。鲍勃·伍德沃德，我知道这样做不对，但我不得不付给一位受访者一块口香糖。另一个孩子尼基坚持要在接受采访前表演魔术。我并没有抱太高期望。

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.990
<v Nicky>And then if I do this--
<v Nicky>然后如果我这样做——

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.440
<v David Kestenbaum>Wait, that worked really well. Wait, that was actually amazing.
<v David Kestenbaum>等等，那招很管用。等等，那真的太神奇了。

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.230
<v Nicky>I know.
<v Nicky>我知道。

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:41.480
<v David Kestenbaum>That was good.
<v David Kestenbaum>那很棒。

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:42.950
<v Nicky>Yeah.
<v Nicky>是的。

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:48.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Nicky then refused to talk about the phone at all. But others clearly used it a lot.
<v David Kestenbaum>尼基随后拒绝再谈论关于电话的事。但其他人显然用了很多次。

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.950
<v Gabriel>You have to tell the tattle-phone the whole story, what happened.
<v Gabriel>你必须告诉告密电话整个故事，发生了什么。

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.690
<v David Kestenbaum>That's Gabriel. This is Deenia.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是加布里埃尔。这是迪尼娅。

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:01.740
<v Deenia>Tell me the whole story. That's all I can remember.
<v Deenia>把整个故事告诉我。我只能记住这些。

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:31.910
<v David Kestenbaum>One girl's parents told me that they were on vacation staying at a hotel, which of course had a landline phone. And their daughter, Simone, said, look, a tattle-phone, as if there was some sort of national tattle-phone network, staffed around the clock by government workers entering everything into a database. My friend's kid Sean, who just turned 5 and who I adore, was the only one who could remember a tattle-worthy event from school in any detail. He whispered it to me. He was clearly kind of excited about this. His friend had kicked him.
<v David Kestenbaum>一个女孩的父母告诉我，他们度假时住在一间酒店，酒店里当然有座机电话。他们的女儿西蒙娜说，看，是一个告密电话，就好像存在某种全国性的告密电话网络，全天候由政府工作人员轮班，将一切录入数据库。我朋友的孩子肖恩，刚满5岁，我非常喜爱他，他是唯一一个能详细记得学校里发生的一件值得告密事件的孩子。他悄悄地告诉了我。他显然对这件事感到有点兴奋。他的朋友踢了他。

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:33.280
<v Sean>(WHISPERING) He kicked me in the penis.
<v Sean>（耳语）他踢了我的阴茎。

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:34.660
<v David Kestenbaum>(WHISPERING) He what?
<v David Kestenbaum>（耳语）他什么？

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:36.630
<v Sean>(WHISPERING) Kicked me in the penis.
<v Sean>（耳语）踢了我的阴茎。

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:41.390
<v David Kestenbaum>(WHISPERING) He kicked you in the penis? Did you tell the tattle-phone?
<v David Kestenbaum>（耳语）他踢了你的阴茎？你告诉告密电话了吗？

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.660
<v Sean>(WHISPERING) That time, we didn't have any tattle-phone.
<v Sean>（耳语）那个时候，我们没有告密电话。

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:46.740
<v David Kestenbaum>(WHISPERING) Oh, you didn't have the tattle-phone?
<v David Kestenbaum>（耳语）哦，你们没有告密电话？

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.400
<v Sean>(WHISPERING) No.
<v Sean>（耳语）没有。

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.270
<v David Kestenbaum>(WHISPERING) If you had the tattle-phone, would you have told it? Yeah? OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>（耳语）如果你有告密电话，你会告诉它吗？是吗？好吧。

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:58.570
<v Ira Glass>It sounds like it might have been an accident. Sean stood up and acted out the whole thing in slow motion for me.
<v Ira Glass>听起来那可能是一次意外。肖恩站起来，慢动作地为我重演了整个过程。

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:00.260
<v Sean>Slow motion.
<v Sean>慢动作。

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:04.340
<v David Kestenbaum>Like it was a scene from an action movie. His friend was trying to kick the ceiling.
<v David Kestenbaum>就像动作片里的一个场景。他的朋友是在试图踢天花板。

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.900
<v Sean>So he went like, [ACTION NOISES].
<v Sean>所以他就像，[动作声]。

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:12.490
<v David Kestenbaum>They should have a security camera in the classroom.
<v David Kestenbaum>他们应该在教室里装个监控摄像头。

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.150
<v Sean>But they don't.
<v Sean>但他们没有。

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:15.680
<v David Kestenbaum>They don't? Just the tattle-phone?
<v David Kestenbaum>他们没有？只有告密电话？

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.210
<v Sean>Mm-hmm.
<v Sean>嗯嗯。

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:27.330
<v David Kestenbaum>A bunch of kids told me they felt good after talking to the tattle-phone, though the why it made them feel better, it took all my interviewing skills. This is Gabriel.
<v David Kestenbaum>一群孩子告诉我，跟告密电话说了之后他们感觉很好，尽管为什么这让他们感觉更好，耗尽了我所有的采访技巧。这是加布里埃尔。

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.180
<v David Kestenbaum>How did you like having the tattle-phone in the classroom?
<v David Kestenbaum>你觉得在教室里放告密电话怎么样？

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.850
<v Gabriel>Good.
<v Gabriel>好。

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:37.090
<v David Kestenbaum>How did it feel after you said something to the tattle-phone?
<v David Kestenbaum>你对告密电话说了什么之后感觉怎么样？

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:38.850
<v Gabriel>Good.
<v Gabriel>好。

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:41.840
<v David Kestenbaum>How come you felt good?
<v David Kestenbaum>为什么你感觉很好？

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:52.430
<v Gabriel>Uh, because-- well, I felt good because I told on the person.
<v Gabriel>呃，因为——嗯，我感觉好是因为我告发了那个人。

00:10:52.430 --> 00:11:04.390
<v David Kestenbaum>One girl told me talking to the tattle-phone felt like eating ice cream.
<v David Kestenbaum>一个女孩告诉我，对着告密电话说话感觉就像吃冰淇淋。

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:24.040
<v David Kestenbaum>It's nice to think that just getting something off your chest can solve a problem, that if you just release the fact of the injustice into the world you feel better. And sure, I think that can be true. But we had the phone in class for a month, and I did notice toward the end, we got fewer messages. They came in regularly, but not with the same energy.
<v David Kestenbaum>想到仅仅是倾诉就能解决问题，想到如果你把不公正的事实释放到世界上，你就会感觉好些，这很美好。当然，我认为那可能是真的。但我们在课堂上放了电话一个月，我确实注意到临近尾声时，我们收到的消息变少了。它们还是会定期出现，但没有了同样的能量。

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:42.160
<v David Kestenbaum>It's possible the novelty was wearing off. I think there's something else going on as well. Before we put the phone in the class, I had it set up in my house, just to make sure it was working. My kids used it a couple of times. And then our younger son Max was complaining that his brother, Auggie, who's a year older, had pinched him.
<v David Kestenbaum>这可能是新鲜感在消退。我想还有别的事情在发生。在我们把电话放进教室之前，我把它放在家里，以确保它能正常工作。我的孩子们用了几次。然后我们的小儿子马克斯抱怨他哥哥奥吉，比他大一岁，掐了他。

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:54.590
<v David Kestenbaum>Tell it to the tattle-phone, I said. It's not working, he told me. I picked the phone up worried that there was some technical glitch. But it was fine. It's working, Max. No, he said, it's not.
<v David Kestenbaum>对着告密电话说吧，我说。它没用，他告诉我。我拿起电话，担心有什么技术故障。但它没问题。它在工作，马克斯。不，他说，没有。

00:11:54.590 --> 00:12:00.990
<v Max>It did not do anything. It doesn't even work to me. It doesn't even do anything.
<v Max>它什么也没做。它甚至对我都没用。它什么都没做。

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:02.890
<v David Kestenbaum>It listened to your tattle.
<v David Kestenbaum>它听到了你的告密。

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.190
<v Max>No, it doesn't.
<v Max>不，它没有。

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.270
<v David Kestenbaum>What do you mean? It listened.
<v David Kestenbaum>你什么意思？它听到了。

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:12.470
<v Max>It didn't. It didn't stop Auggie pinching me.
<v Max>它没有。它没能阻止奥吉掐我。

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:26.710
<v David Kestenbaum>It didn't stop Auggie pinching me. I know, Max. I know. Sometimes you want more than just to speak. You want actual justice.
<v David Kestenbaum>它没能阻止奥吉掐我。我知道，马克斯。我知道。有时你想要的不仅仅是诉说。你想要的是真正的正义。

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:28.190
<v Ira Glass>Thank you, David.
<v Ira Glass>谢谢你，大卫。

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:29.470
<v David Kestenbaum>You're welcome.
<v David Kestenbaum>不客气。

00:12:29.470 --> 00:13:17.880
<v Ira Glass>And that brings us to-- like, what are we, 12 minutes into the program-- it brings us to what we are doing today on our show. Today we have people, some of them young, some of them old, wanting justice, feeling a sense of injustice, on things as big as the constitutional foundation of our entire country and things as small as the question of whether a ball is in or out. And in all those cases, people young and old not trusting the system to deliver justice. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这把我们带到了——我们在节目中过了多久了，12分钟？——它把我们带到了我们今天在节目中要做的事情。今天我们有一些人，有些年轻，有些年长，他们渴望正义，感受到一种不公正，事情大到我们整个国家的宪法基础，小到关于球是在界内还是界外的问题。而在所有这些案例中，无论是年轻人还是老年人都不信任体系能伸张正义。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。请继续收听。

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:42.620
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Hoop Reams. The journalist Michael Lewis, who wrote Moneyball and The Big Short and lots of other books and who contributes to our program now and then, he was looking around at what's going on in America these days. And he noticed that one way you can describe the current moment that we're all living through is that Americans don't trust the refs-- in all walks of life. They don't trust their impartiality.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，篮球梦想。记者迈克尔·刘易斯，他写了《点球成金》、《大空头》以及许多其他书籍，并不时为我们的节目供稿。他一直在观察美国当下的局势。他注意到，你描述我们所有人正在经历的当下的方式之一，就是美国人不信任裁判——在各行各业都是如此。他们不信任裁判的公正性。

00:13:42.620 --> 00:14:08.510
<v Ira Glass>I'm talking about police, Supreme Court justices, journalists, the people who regulate the banks and Wall Street and student loans, the people setting medical costs, judges. So many people today feel the system is rigged. I mean, Bernie Sanders and Donald Trump both ran on that. So many people feel that the figures of authority, charged with enforcing rules impartially, keeping everybody on a level playing field, that they're failing at their jobs. It's not fair.
<v Ira Glass>我说的是警察、最高法院大法官、记者，监管银行、华尔街和学生贷款的人，设定医疗成本的人，法官。今天有太多人觉得这个体系是被操纵的。我的意思是，伯尼·桑德斯和唐纳德·特朗普竞选时都主张这一点。有太多人觉得那些肩负着公正执法、让每个人处于平等竞争环境的权威人物，他们的工作失败了。这不公平。

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:31.040
<v Ira Glass>Michael Lewis has not published this reporting that he's been doing on this in a new book. He's decided to make it into a podcast. They launched this week. It's called Against The Rules. And Michael has adapted a version of their very first episode for us to play for you right now. The first episode is about people not trusting the refs in the most literal sense of that. It's about basketball referees. Here's Michael Lewis.
<v Ira Glass>迈克尔·刘易斯并没有将他一直在进行的这篇报道发表成新书。他决定把它做成一档播客。他们本周上线了。节目叫《规则之外》。迈克尔为我们改编了他们第一集的内容，现在播放给大家听。第一集是关于人们在最字面的意义上不信任裁判的故事。是关于篮球裁判的。这是迈克尔·刘易斯。

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:48.440
<v Michael Lewis>For me, this story really begins with my 11-year-old son, Walker. He plays on a basketball team run by a Japanese Buddhist temple. My son isn't a Buddhist, though most of the time he could pass for one. He has no conflict with his teachers or his classmates or his Japanese Buddhist teammates.
<v Michael Lewis>对我来说，这个故事确实始于我11岁的儿子沃克。他效力于一支由日本佛寺运营的篮球队。我儿子不是佛教徒，尽管大多数时候他看起来很像。他和老师、同学或日本佛教徒队友之间没有什么冲突。

00:14:48.440 --> 00:15:21.620
<v Michael Lewis>I wouldn't say his mind is exactly pure, but usually it's calm. The exception is when he deals with refs. Even in what amounts to a Buddhist basketball game, anytime a ref blows the whistle on him, he throws up his arms in astonishment. Then he jumps up and down with his little fists balled up and his mouth clenched tight, so everyone knows just how much injustice he's suffering. Then he marches off with a scowl. And he doesn't get over it. After a game this season, he gets into the car and starts bitching and moaning all over again.
<v Michael Lewis>我不会说他的头脑绝对纯洁，但通常都很冷静。例外是在他面对裁判的时候。即使是在某种相当于佛教徒的篮球比赛中，只要裁判对他吹哨，他就会惊愕地举起双臂。然后他会挥舞着小拳头，紧咬牙关蹦蹦跳跳，好让每个人都知道他受了多大的不公正待遇。然后他会气冲冲地走开。而且他对此耿耿于怀。这个赛季的一场比赛后，他上了车，开始不停地抱怨和呻吟。

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:24.260
<v Michael Lewis>How did it make you feel when the ref made those calls?
<v Michael Lewis>裁判做出那些判罚时，你感觉怎么样？

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.760
<v Walker>Very mad.
<v Walker>非常生气。

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.580
<v Michael Lewis>Do you feel any better now?
<v Michael Lewis>你现在感觉好点了吗？

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.350
<v Walker>No.
<v Walker>没有。

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.930
<v Michael Lewis>Tell me how it feels.
<v Michael Lewis>告诉我那是种什么感觉。

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:41.870
<v Walker>It feels like someone keeps poking you in the back of the shoulder and then saying, foul, foul, foul, foul.
<v Walker>感觉就像有人不断地戳你的后肩，然后说，犯规，犯规，犯规，犯规。

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:43.730
<v Michael Lewis>Have you ever fouled out in your career?
<v Michael Lewis>你的生涯中有过被罚出场吗？

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:44.660
<v Walker>No.
<v Walker>没有。

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:47.060
<v Michael Lewis>Did you know that you were at risk of fouling out?
<v Michael Lewis>你知道你有被罚出场的风险吗？

00:15:47.060 --> 00:16:00.800
<v Walker>Yes, but I also knew if I did, it would be unfair because I knew that he was calling the stupidest fouls. He'll look back and say, oh, I was being a huge-- asshole.
<v Walker>知道，但我也知道如果我被罚出场，那是不公平的，因为我知道他在吹一些极其愚蠢的犯规。他会回头说，噢，我当时表现得像个超级——混蛋。

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:08.160
<v Basketball Announcer>Klay with the steal. Klay in a footrace. Cousins trying to catch him. He can't! Klay with a very deep three.
<v Basketball Announcer>克莱抢断。克莱在赛跑。考辛斯试图追上他。他做不到！克莱超远三分。

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:16.330
<v Michael Lewis>The thing is, I know why my son does what he does. He thinks he's Klay Thompson, the all-star shooting guard of the Golden State Warriors.
<v Michael Lewis>问题在于，我知道我儿子为什么要那样做。他以为他是克莱·汤普森，金州勇士队的全明星得分后卫。

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:20.010
<v Basketball Announcer>They reset back to Thompson. Three-pointer-- bang.
<v Basketball Announcer>他们重新把球传回给汤普森。三分球——进了。

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:31.060
<v Michael Lewis>When Klay hits a three, Klay pounds his chest and points to the sky. And so when Walker Lewis hits a three, he too pounds his chest and points to the sky.
<v Michael Lewis>当克莱投进三分时，克莱会拍拍胸口并指向天空。所以当沃克·刘易斯投进三分时，他也拍拍胸口指向天空。

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:37.930
<v Basketball Announcer>And I think we have a technical foul as well. On Klay-- very unusual.
<v Basketball Announcer>我想我们还有一个技术犯规。判给克莱——非常罕见。

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:48.370
<v Michael Lewis>When Klay is called for a foul, he scowls and throws up his arms in astonishment, and sometimes even says something to the ref that gets him slapped with a technical foul.
<v Michael Lewis>当克莱被吹犯规时，他会皱起眉头，惊愕地举起手臂，有时甚至对裁判说些什么，导致他被判技术犯规。

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:52.520
<v Basketball Announcer>He only had one during the entire regular season.
<v Basketball Announcer>他在整个常规赛期间只得到过一次。

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:58.490
<v Michael Lewis>And Klay's the famously most laid back all-star in the entire National Basketball Association.
<v Michael Lewis>而且克莱是整个国家篮球协会中众所周知的最随和的全明星球员。

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:04.550
<v Ramona Shelburne>Something has happened in the relationship between referees and players over the last, I guess, year or two.
<v Ramona Shelburne>在过去的一两年里，裁判和球员之间的关系发生了一些变化。

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:09.170
<v Michael Lewis>That's Ramona Shelburne. She's covered the NBA for the last decade for ESPN.
<v Michael Lewis>那是拉莫娜·谢尔本。她在ESPN报道NBA已经十年了。

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:12.250
<v Ramona Shelburne>It's been, quite frankly, ugly this year.
<v Ramona Shelburne>说实话，今年情况很糟糕。

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:19.150
<v Michael Lewis>One of the Warriors head-butted a ref. Another chucked his disgusting mouth guard off a ref's chest.
<v Michael Lewis>一名勇士队球员用头撞了裁判。另一名球员把恶心的牙套扔在了裁判的胸口上。

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.400
<v Basketball Announcer>And Curry has been thrown out of the game.
<v Basketball Announcer>库里也被赶出了比赛。

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:31.070
<v Ramona Shelburne>And some of the stuff I've seen, I mean, when Draymond Green is getting fined for calling Lauren Holtkamp a-- can we cuss on your podcast?
<v Ramona Shelburne>而且我看到的一些事情，我的意思是，当德雷蒙德·格林因为称呼劳伦·霍尔特坎普为——我们可以在你的播客里骂人吗？

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.510
<v Michael Lewis>I think we already have.
<v Michael Lewis>我想我们已经骂过了。

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:53.390
<v Ramona Shelburne>You know, a fucking bitch-- like, when he is saying that to a female referee, man, that's next level. And I haven't seen that before this season. It's the stars now who are really pushing the issue, right? It's Kevin Durant getting thrown out of games. It's Steph Curry getting thrown out.
<v Ramona Shelburne>你知道，一个他妈的婊子——当他那样称呼一位女裁判时，伙计，那真是太离谱了。在这个赛季之前我没见过这种情况。现在是球星们在推动这个问题，对吧？是凯文·杜兰特被赶出比赛。是斯蒂芬·库里被赶出比赛。

00:17:53.390 --> 00:18:00.820
<v Basketball Announcer>And the Golden State Warriors have completely unraveled.
<v Basketball Announcer>而且金州勇士队彻底失控了。

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:22.290
<v Michael Lewis>Last year, bad behavior got various Golden State Warriors tossed out of 10 different basketball games. Kevin Durant, their best player, got thrown out of five-- four more than he'd been thrown out of his entire career up to that point. The men who coach the stars aren't much better. Even Steve Kerr, the Warriors' famously decent, civic-minded coach--
<v Michael Lewis>去年，糟糕的行为导致各种金州勇士队球员在10场不同的篮球比赛中被罚出场。凯文·杜兰特，他们最好的球员，被罚出场5次——比他职业生涯至今的总和还要多出4次。执教这些球星的人也好不到哪儿去。即使是史蒂夫·科尔，勇士队出了名的正派、有公民意识的教练——

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:25.270
<v Steve Kerr>I can snap. I can completely lose it.
<v Steve Kerr>我会抓狂。我会彻底失控。

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:43.500
<v Michael Lewis>If you back away from the Golden State Warriors, I mean, they're exemplars of the way people should behave. Especially your stars, they're, like, impeccably behaved people. I'm so pleased to have my son emulating your players. The only time they have problems really is with referees.
<v Michael Lewis>如果你退后一步观察金州勇士队，他们是人们应该如何表现的典范。尤其是你的球星们，他们表现得非常无可挑剔。我很高兴我的儿子效仿你们的球员。他们真正遇到问题的唯一时候就是面对裁判。

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:44.380
<v Steve Kerr>Yeah.
<v Steve Kerr>是的。

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:49.230
<v Michael Lewis>I can't imagine you in your life have another relationship like you have with referees.
<v Michael Lewis>我无法想象你在生活中还会和裁判有其他关系。

00:18:49.230 --> 00:19:10.600
<v Steve Kerr>No, you're right. You're right. I would never say the things that I do to referees to a person in normal life. It happens two or three times a year. I've been caught on camera, you know, MF-ing a ref. And, you know, my daughter will send me a text, like Dad, what are you doing? It's all over Twitter. I can read your lips. This is embarrass-- and I'm embarrassed.
<v Steve Kerr>不，你是对的。你是对的。在正常生活中，我绝不会对别人说那些我对裁判说的话。这种情况一年发生两三次。我被摄像机拍到过，你知道，我骂裁判。而且，我的女儿会发短信给我，说爸爸，你在干嘛？推特上到处都是。我能读你的唇语。这太尴尬了——而我也感到很尴尬。

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:24.790
<v Steve Kerr>So why is that? It's a sense of right or wrong. You know, I feel like there's this personal offense, like something unfair is happening.
<v Steve Kerr>所以这是为什么呢？这是一种对与错的观念。你知道，我感觉这是一种个人冒犯，好像有什么不公平的事情正在发生。

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:57.460
<v Michael Lewis>The players get fined if they talk about the refs, so we won't be talking to them. But it's not just them or their coaches or my son who are treating refs in ways they'd never treat an ordinary person. It's the fans in every arena who spend meaningful amounts of their time looking for the refs' mistakes on the JumboTron. It's the cable sports channels playing and replaying the refs' mistakes so their viewers can tweet and retweet about them. It's this entire infrastructure, seemingly built to focus attention on whatever mistakes the refs make for the sole purpose of generating outrage.
<v Michael Lewis>球员如果谈论裁判会被罚款，所以我们不会跟他们谈论。但不仅仅是他们或他们的教练或我的儿子在以一种他们绝不会对待普通人的方式对待裁判。在每个竞技场里，都有球迷花费大量的时间在场馆大屏幕上寻找裁判的失误。是有线体育频道反复播放裁判的失误，让观众在推特上转发和评论。这是一个庞大的基础设施，似乎专门为了聚焦裁判犯下的任何错误而建立，唯一的目的就是制造愤怒。

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:02.090
<v Crowd>(CHANTING) Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck!
<v Crowd>（呼喊）裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:19.210
<v Michael Lewis>If there's one thing that unites Americans just now, it's their belief that the refs suck-- which is weird, because they almost certainly suck less than ever before. Before you hate me for saying that, give me a moment to show why.
<v Michael Lewis>如果说现在有什么能团结美国人的话，那就是他们认为裁判烂透了——这很奇怪，因为他们几乎可以肯定比以往任何时候都做得好。在你因为我这么说而讨厌我之前，让我花点时间展示一下原因。

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:46.130
<v Michael Lewis>So we're walking across a parking lot in Secaucus, New Jersey. And there are chain hotels and motels in someone's idea of a mall. And we're surrounded on all sides by freeways. It's like what people think about when they tell jokes about New Jersey. And we're approaching a four-story, rectangular, otherwise nondescript concrete building.
<v Michael Lewis>我们在新泽西州锡考克斯的一个停车场里走。周围是连锁酒店和汽车旅馆，位于某人构思的商场里。我们四面被高速公路包围。就像人们讲新泽西笑话时想到的那样。我们正在接近一栋四层楼高的矩形建筑，外观普通。

00:20:46.130 --> 00:21:16.340
<v Michael Lewis>There's a discreet little sign here that says NBA and shows a logo with a basketball player. Inside, a recent concession to the world we live in-- the Replay Center, a place where basketball referees review the calls made by other basketball referees in real time, to minimize referee error. The Replay Center was built to persuade people that life was fair.
<v Michael Lewis>这里有个不显眼的小牌子，上面写着NBA，还有一个篮球运动员的标志。里面，是为我们生活的世界所做的近期让步——回放中心，一个篮球裁判实时审核其他篮球裁判判罚的地方，旨在最大限度地减少裁判失误。回放中心的建立是为了让人们相信生活是公平的。

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:20.360
<v Michael Lewis>And the door is locked.
<v Michael Lewis>而且门是锁着的。

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:33.050
<v Michael Lewis>Someone eventually unlocks the door and leads me down a hall filled with a lot of old basketball stuff-- jerseys and bobbleheads and basketballs and posters of Michael Jordan. The Replay Center was the ultimate man cave--
<v Michael Lewis>最后有人打开了门，领我走进一个走廊，里面摆满了许多老旧的篮球物品——球衣、摇头娃娃、篮球和迈克尔·乔丹的海报。回放中心是终极男性洞穴——

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:34.580
<v Joe Borgia>Ultimate man cave, right?
<v Joe Borgia>终极男性洞穴，对吧？

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:37.240
<v Michael Lewis>It's also the latest weapon in the battle for fairness.
<v Michael Lewis>它也是追求公平战斗中的最新武器。

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:40.090
<v Michael Lewis>So this place, just on first view, is amazing.
<v Michael Lewis>所以这个地方，第一眼看上去，真是令人惊叹。

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:40.490
<v Joe Borgia>It really is.
<v Joe Borgia>确实如此。

00:21:40.490 --> 00:22:03.920
<v Michael Lewis>It's wall-to-wall screens, 110 of them. What's on them is whatever is captured by all the cameras in 29 NBA arenas across the country. They may have a screen somewhere with scores on it, but I didn't see it. And they're all muted. What you hear is referees staring at basketball games. What you see is nothing but angles on professional basketball courts.
<v Michael Lewis>它布满了屏幕，一共有110个。上面显示的是全国29个NBA场馆内所有摄像机捕捉到的画面。他们可能在某个地方有个显示比分的屏幕，但我没看见。而且它们全部都是静音的。你听到的是裁判盯着篮球比赛的声音。你看到的是专业篮球场上无尽的角度。

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:07.130
<v Joe Borgia>Nobody's ever walked in here and walked out and said, this place sucks.
<v Joe Borgia>从来没有人走进来走出去后说，这个地方糟透了。

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:25.940
<v Michael Lewis>That's Joe Borgia, who's run the center since 2014. Before he volunteered for Secaucus duty, he refereed NBA games. His father was an NBA ref before him. With a break in the late '60s, a Borgia has been reffing professional basketball games since 1946.
<v Michael Lewis>那是乔·博尔吉亚，他自2014年以来一直负责这个中心。在志愿服务锡考克斯之前，他曾担任NBA裁判。他的父亲在他之前也是一名NBA裁判。除了60年代末的短暂中断，博尔吉亚家族自1946年以来一直有人在执裁职业篮球比赛。

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:32.410
<v Michael Lewis>If you went back to your dad at the beginning of his career and said, this is what it's going to look like, what do you think he'd have said?
<v Michael Lewis>如果你回到你父亲职业生涯的早期说，未来会变成这样，你认为他会怎么说？

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:36.920
<v Joe Borgia>If I told him we would have replay, he'd turn over in his grave, forget about a replay center.
<v Joe Borgia>如果我告诉他我们会使用回放，他会从坟墓里跳出来，别提什么回放中心了。

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:37.790
<v Michael Lewis>Is that right?
<v Michael Lewis>是这样吗？

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:38.770
<v Joe Borgia>Oh, absolutely.
<v Joe Borgia>噢，绝对。

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:52.750
<v Michael Lewis>You see, the refs used to insist on their authority. And everyone agreed that there was no better way to ensure the fairness of the game than to let the ref play god. The Replay Center is an admission that the ref is not god, that he makes mistakes.
<v Michael Lewis>你看，裁判过去坚持他们的权威。每个人都同意，确保比赛公平的最好方法就是让裁判扮演上帝。回放中心是一个承认，承认裁判不是上帝，他会犯错。

00:22:52.750 --> 00:23:03.020
<v Joe Borgia>I think the mention of replay, none of us liked it when we first heard it. It's a necessary evil. It's necessary. You have to have it today.
<v Joe Borgia>我认为提到回放时，我们刚听说时都不喜欢它。这是必要的恶。它是必要的。今天你必须拥有它。

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:15.710
<v Michael Lewis>Everything is taped now. Everyone pays more attention to the referees' mistakes. So the NBA has to as well. Now, when a ref thinks he might have screwed up some call or didn't get a good look at the action--
<v Michael Lewis>现在一切都被录了下来。每个人都更加关注裁判的失误。所以NBA也必须关注。现在，当裁判认为他可能搞砸了某次判罚，或者没有看清楚动作——

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:17.540
<v Joe Borgia>Watch the referee on the bottom of the screen.
<v Joe Borgia>看屏幕底部的裁判。

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:19.400
<v Michael Lewis>--he twirls his fingers in the air.
<v Michael Lewis>——他会在空中转动手指。

