00:23:35.140 --> 00:24:08.130
<v Michael Lewis>The refs here sit dressed in black staring at screens, waiting for a signal from somewhere in America. The end of games is when they get most involved, because that's when fans and coaches and players are most likely to accuse some ref of having made the mistake that changed the outcome. Of course, a mistake at the beginning has just as much effect on a game as a mistake at the end, but the end is what people notice and get outraged about. So the justice at the end of the game must be more exact than it is at the beginning.
<v Michael Lewis>这里的裁判身着黑色服装，盯着屏幕，等待着来自美国某处的信号。比赛末段是他们参与最多的时候，因为那时球迷、教练和球员最有可能指责某个裁判犯了改变结果的错误。当然，开始时的错误和结束时的错误对比赛有同样的影响，但人们注意并感到愤怒的是结尾。因此，比赛结束时的正义必须比开始时更精确。

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:26.150
<v Michael Lewis>These Replay Center refs have video technicians with them, who can freeze a moment on screen, then zoom out or zoom in so that the entire screen contains only a player's fingertips or his toes. Here you just scroll through tiny slivers of the game, not the game itself. The sliver is where injustices might occur.
<v Michael Lewis>这些回放中心的裁判配有视频技术员，他们可以将屏幕上的瞬间冻结，然后放大或缩小，以至于整个屏幕只包含一名球员的指尖或脚趾。在这里你只是浏览比赛的小碎片，而不是比赛本身。这些碎片就是可能发生不公正的地方。

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:31.650
<v Joe Borgia>I mean, goodness gracious, if you don't have slow motion in here or freeze frame, it's very difficult.
<v Joe Borgia>我的意思是，天哪，如果你在这里没有慢动作或定格，那真的非常困难。

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:46.040
<v Michael Lewis>Of course, in slow motion, you can see things that the naked eye misses. Magicians sometimes perform during halftimes of the NBA games. When Joe Borgia slows it down, he can see how they do their tricks. It's kind of the same thing with the players.
<v Michael Lewis>当然，在慢动作中，你可以看到肉眼错过的东西。魔术师有时会在NBA比赛的中场休息时表演。当乔·博尔吉亚放慢速度时，他就能看清他们是如何表演把戏的。球员的情况也是一样的。

