00:28:41.690 --> 00:29:07.840
<v Michael Lewis>He's insisted that referees be physically fit so they can get into position to see all the plays. While everyone else in America is getting fatter, the refs are getting buff. They're also now getting new feedback on all their bad calls. Silver decided to publish the mistakes made by every ref in the last two minutes of every game so everyone could see them. He gives the teams and the refs a private document listing every refereeing mistake.
<v Michael Lewis>他坚持要求裁判保持良好的身体素质，以便他们能到位看到所有的比赛。当美国其他人都变得越来越胖时，裁判们却变得越来越壮。他们现在还针对自己所有的错误判罚得到了新的反馈。肖华决定公布每场比赛最后两分钟裁判犯下的错误，以便每个人都能看到。他给球队和裁判一份私密文件，列出每一个裁判失误。

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:23.590
<v Michael Lewis>All this new data on refs means that we and they know all sorts of strange things about their minds. For instance, we now know that their calls have tended to favor whichever team is losing. Their calls also favor the home team. Some large part of home court advantage is just the refs.
<v Michael Lewis>关于裁判的所有这些新数据意味着我们和他们都知道关于他们思维的各种奇怪的事情。例如，我们现在知道他们的判罚倾向于支持落后的一方。他们的判罚也偏向主队。主场优势很大一部分只是裁判因素。

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:39.610
<v Michael Lewis>The analytics department of the Houston Rockets has even done a study that shows that the home team that gets the best calls is the Utah Jazz. Why Utah? Who knows? But you can be sure that someone will figure that out. There's now basically a small army of geeks analyzing all this new data.
<v Michael Lewis>休斯顿火箭队的分析部门甚至做了一项研究，显示获得最好判罚的主队是犹他爵士队。为什么是犹他？谁知道呢？但你可以肯定会有人找出原因。现在基本上有一支小型的书呆子大军在分析所有这些新数据。

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:44.560
<v Justin Wolfers>Look, I don't really like writing papers about sports. I'd prefer to write about the economy.
<v Justin Wolfers>看，我真的不喜欢写关于体育的论文。我更倾向于写关于经济的。

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:50.860
<v Michael Lewis>That's Justin Wolfers, a behavioral economist at the University of Michigan, and the co-author of a paper about NBA refs.
<v Michael Lewis>那是贾斯汀·沃尔弗斯，密歇根大学的行为经济学家，也是一篇关于NBA裁判论文的合著者。

00:29:50.860 --> 00:30:09.420
<v Justin Wolfers>But the thing is, this is a domain where the NBA referees have tremendous incentives not to make the wrong call. Every error they make is tracked. Those errors determine whether they get more games. Those games determine how much they get paid. This is arguably the most analyzed workforce in the country.
<v Justin Wolfers>但问题是，这是一个NBA裁判极度没有动力去做错误判罚的领域。他们犯下的每一个错误都被追踪。这些错误决定了他们是否能获得更多的比赛。那些比赛决定了他们能挣多少钱。这可以说是全国受分析程度最高的劳动力群体。

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:29.730
<v Michael Lewis>Basketball referees are now picked apart in ways that not long ago would have seemed preposterous-- not just for the fairness of their calls but for their unconscious behavior. Wolfers took data from over a decade of NBA basketball games, more than 250,000 of them. Then he set out to look for evidence of the refs' racial bias.
<v Michael Lewis>篮球裁判现在被分析的方式在不久前看来是荒谬的——不仅是因为他们判罚的公平性，还因为他们的无意识行为。沃尔弗斯使用了十多年NBA篮球比赛的数据，超过25万场比赛的数据。然后他着手寻找裁判种族偏见的证据。

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:53.590
<v Justin Wolfers>The question here isn't whether people are anti-black or anti-white but whether there's an in-group bias. So if a predominately black team is playing and the refereeing crew is predominantly white, are there more fouls called against them than on nights when the same team is playing with a predominantly black refereeing crew? And it turns out, the answer is yes.
<v Justin Wolfers>这里的问题不是人们是否反黑或反白，而是是否存在群体内偏见。所以如果一个以黑人为主的球队在比赛，而裁判组以白人为主，那么与同一支球队在以黑人为主的裁判组执裁的夜晚相比，他们被吹的犯规是否更多？结果证明，答案是肯定的。

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:59.480
<v Michael Lewis>Wolfers wrote his paper back in 2007, before this new age of referee transparency.
<v Michael Lewis>沃尔弗斯在2007年写了他的论文，在裁判透明度这个新时代之前。

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:10.050
<v Justin Wolfers>Well, it was a bit of a lesson for me. You can probably tell by my accent, Michael, I'm an Australian. You know, I thought it was an interesting piece of social science. It turned out The New York Times put it on the front page.
<v Justin Wolfers>嗯，这对我来说是个教训。你可能通过我的口音听出来了，迈克尔，我是澳大利亚人。你知道，我以为这是一个有趣的社会科学片段。结果《纽约时报》把它放在了头版。

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:18.760
<v Michael Lewis>And the NBA wasn't happy. The commissioner at the time attacked the study and embarrassed the league by trying and failing to refute its findings.
<v Michael Lewis>而NBA并不高兴。当时的总裁攻击了这项研究，并试图推翻其结论但失败了，让联盟感到尴尬。

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:22.210
<v Man>This morning we'll hear from the NBA commissioner, David Stern.
<v Man>今天早上我们将听到NBA总裁大卫·斯特恩的发言。

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:30.530
<v David Stern>Our referees are the most reviewed, most ranked, and most rated. And that's why we take exception to what The Times did here.
<v David Stern>我们的裁判是最受审查、排名和评级的。这就是为什么我们对《时报》在这里所做的事情提出异议。

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:50.290
<v Michael Lewis>That's Stern on NPR in 2007. The result of all this coverage, every single referee was made aware of his unconscious bias. When the dust settled, Justin Wolfers was curious to know if his paper had had any effect. He made another study of referees after the controversy he'd created. And guess what.
<v Michael Lewis>那是2007年斯特恩在NPR上的发言。所有这些报道的结果是，每一位裁判都意识到了自己的无意识偏见。当尘埃落定后，贾斯汀·沃尔弗斯好奇地想知道他的论文是否产生了任何影响。他在引发争议后又对裁判进行了另一项研究。你猜怎么着。

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:55.110
<v Justin Wolfers>The most recent study that we did seems to suggest that that form of racial bias has gone away.
<v Justin Wolfers>我们所做的最新研究似乎表明，那种形式的种族偏见已经消失了。

00:31:55.110 --> 00:32:27.850
<v Michael Lewis>For a while anyway. He has no idea why. Maybe simply making the refs aware of the problem was enough to correct it. By the way, the NBA disputes this study too. But in the end, this became a case study-- not in ref ineptitude but in ref reform. NBA refs have now achieved what police forces can only dream of, though the refs have no choice. The world's now too good at seeing their mistakes.
<v Michael Lewis>至少有一段时间。他不知道为什么。也许仅仅让裁判意识到这个问题就足以纠正它了。顺便说一下，NBA也质疑这项研究。但最终，这成为了一个案例研究——不是关于裁判的无能，而是关于裁判的改革。NBA裁判现在实现了警察部队只能梦想的目标，尽管裁判别无选择。现在全世界都太擅长发现他们的错误了。

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:41.350
<v Michael Lewis>Look, there's no way any basketball referee is going to be perfect. But there's also no way these refs are anything but more accurate than they've ever been. I mean, even home court advantage means less than it used to. And yet these refs are treated as if they're trying to rig the games.
<v Michael Lewis>看，没有任何篮球裁判是完美的。但也绝不可能这些裁判做得比以往任何时候都差。我的意思是，即使是主场优势也比以前少多了。然而这些裁判被对待得好像他们试图操纵比赛一样。

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:50.800
<v Crowd>(CHANTING) Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck! Ref, you suck!
<v Crowd>（呼喊）裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！裁判，你烂透了！

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:57.100
<v Michael Lewis>The sound of those 18,000 people screaming at you or booing you, does it sound any different than it sounded when you started?
<v Michael Lewis>听到那18000人对你尖叫或嘘你，它听起来和你开始时有什么不同吗？

00:32:57.100 --> 00:33:07.260
<v Monty Mccutchen>Yeah. There's a little more anger involved. And, you know, it used to be, sort of, of the garden variety. You're terrible, you suck, any of those kinds of terms.
<v Monty Mccutchen>是的。涉及了更多的愤怒。而且，你知道，过去那种通常是普通类型的。你很糟糕，你烂透了，任何这类词汇。

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:16.120
<v Michael Lewis>That's Monty McCutchen, who started reffing in 1993, and so got to see firsthand the effect that the arrival of the internet has had on referees.
<v Michael Lewis>那是蒙蒂·麦卡琴，他在1993年开始执裁，因此亲眼目睹了互联网的到来对裁判产生的影响。

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:25.770
<v Monty Mccutchen>A few years back, twitter exploded in game five of the Miami Heat and Oklahoma City Thunder finals from years ago.
<v Monty Mccutchen>几年前，推特在热火和雷霆的总决赛第五场中爆发了。

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:36.220
<v Michael Lewis>One of the players hit a three point shot. McCutchen was giving the signal for three and running to beat the players back to the other end of the court when he realized he had lost his balance.
<v Michael Lewis>一名球员投中了一个三分球。麦卡琴正在给出三分信号，并跑向另一端以赶在球员之前，这时他意识到自己失去了平衡。

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:48.460
<v Monty Mccutchen>Falling for a referee, it doesn't get much more embarrassing than that. It's bad. People are on you pretty good, you know. And I'm trying to hold it up, and Erik was on the sideline. And he sees me fall.
<v Monty Mccutchen>对于裁判来说，跌倒没有什么比这更尴尬的了。这很糟糕。人们对你很严厉，你知道。我正试图支撑住，埃里克在场边。他看到我摔倒了。

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:51.510
<v Michael Lewis>Erik is Erik Spoelstra, the Miami Heat coach.
<v Michael Lewis>埃里克是埃里克·斯波尔斯特拉，迈阿密热火队的教练。

00:33:51.510 --> 00:34:15.280
<v Monty Mccutchen>And he sort of just stood onto the floor and braced me. And our arms sort of interlocked as he braced me, righted me. I yell out, thanks, as I go on by, and I run on down to the baseline. After the game, Twitter exploded that the NBA is rigged. If you don't believe that watch Monty McCutchen give a high five to Erik Spoelstra after this three.
<v Monty Mccutchen>他只是站到地板上扶住了我。当我们扶住我时，我们的手臂缠在一起，把我扶正了。我路过时大喊一声谢谢，然后跑向底线。赛后，推特上关于NBA是被操纵的言论爆发了。如果你不相信，看看蒙蒂·麦卡琴在这个三分球后和埃里克·斯波尔斯特拉击掌。

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:29.650
<v Monty Mccutchen>And the reality of that was just so far and away wrong up against this real human moment of just a reactionary thing that a good person does for another good person. You see someone falling, you help them not fall.
<v Monty Mccutchen>而那件事的真相与那种好的对他人的那种人反应性的真实人类时刻相比，实在是太错误了。你看到有人摔倒，你帮助他们不摔倒。

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:36.880
<v Michael Lewis>He says that when he started out reffing, no one would have even noticed. And if they did, it wouldn't have mattered. That's changed.
<v Michael Lewis>他说当他刚开始执裁时，没人会注意。即便他们注意到了，也无所谓。这种情况已经改变了。

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:54.670
<v Monty Mccutchen>Mistakes get made as NBA referees. And they're just mistakes. But they often get interpreted in this wider scheme of thought process that a lot of our cultures is dealing with right now, which is, there must be a fix in. There must be a reason for this.
<v Monty Mccutchen>作为NBA裁判，错误总是会犯的。它们只是错误。但它们经常被解释为我们许多文化现在正在处理的更广泛的过程思维方案的一部分，那就是，肯定有什么猫腻。肯定有个原因。

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:59.020
<v Michael Lewis>Do you think the world is looking for a fix more now than it was when you started?
<v Michael Lewis>你认为世界现在比你刚开始的时候更在寻找猫腻吗？

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:05.230
<v Monty Mccutchen>I think that the t-shirt I saw recently has merit-- "I'm not saying it's your fault. I'm saying I'm blaming you."
<v Monty Mccutchen>我认为我最近看到的T恤很有道理——“我不是说那是你的错。我说的是我在责怪你。”

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:05.920
<v Michael Lewis>You feel abused.
<v Michael Lewis>你感到被虐待了。

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:17.750
<v Monty Mccutchen>Yeah. Now, you know, people do their research. Things are out there on the internet. They know your record with their team. There's all these sites on all these different--
<v Monty Mccutchen>是的。现在，你知道，人们做他们的研究。网上的东西很多。他们知道你对他们球队的记录。有所有这些网站在所有这些不同的——

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:19.390
<v Michael Lewis>Do they know personal things about you?
<v Michael Lewis>他们知道关于你的私人信息吗？

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:29.860
<v Monty Mccutchen>Oh, sure. Of course, some death threats are made from time to time in playoff series, and you'll get security all the way to both the hotel and then the hotel the next morning out to the airport. That's a--
<v Monty Mccutchen>噢，当然。当然，季后赛中不时会有死亡威胁，你会有安保一直送到酒店，然后第二天早上从酒店送到机场。这是一个——

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:31.030
<v Michael Lewis>You have security to the hotel?
<v Michael Lewis>你有送到酒店的安保？

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:39.550
<v Monty Mccutchen>Not every night, but when those threats are a known factor-- it has happened in my career-- security to your car is mandatory every night.
<v Monty Mccutchen>不是每天晚上，但当那些威胁是一个已知的因素时——在我的职业生涯中确实发生过——每天晚上去你的车的安保是强制性的。

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:47.620
<v Michael Lewis>And at some point you feel this question rising up in you. In me it rose up while I was talking to Ramona Shelburne, the ESPN reporter.
<v Michael Lewis>在某个时刻，你感到这个问题在你心中升起。在我这里，它是在我和ESPN记者拉莫娜·谢尔本交谈时升起的。

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:49.600
<v Michael Lewis>Why would anybody want to be a ref?
<v Michael Lewis>为什么有人想当裁判？

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:58.950
<v Ramona Shelburne>Seriously. I wonder that too, man. I just-- you know, they're not allowed to say anything. They're not allowed to explain themselves. They're not allowed to defend themselves.
<v Ramona Shelburne>说真的。我也很好奇，伙计。我只是——你知道，他们不允许说任何话。他们不允许解释自己。他们不允许为自己辩护。

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:16.180
<v Michael Lewis>Look, obviously they get paid. They start at 150 grand a year. And if they're great at their job and work extra games, they can make as much as 500. But there are lots of ways to get paid without spending half your life in hotel rooms and the other half being insulted by arenas filled with crazy people.
<v Michael Lewis>看，很明显他们拿工资。他们起薪一年15万美元。如果他们工作出色且多执裁比赛，他们能挣到50万美元。但有很多挣钱的方法，而不需要把你的一半生命花在酒店房间里，另一半在挤满疯子的竞技场里被侮辱。

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:19.400
<v Michael Lewis>Do you think the refereeing has gotten worse or better?
<v Michael Lewis>你认为裁判工作变差了还是变好了？

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:20.670
<v Ramona Shelburne>I actually think it's gotten better.
<v Ramona Shelburne>我实际上认为它变好了。

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:52.890
<v Michael Lewis>Of course it's gotten better. How could it not have? The mystery is why the stars and the coaches and the fans act as if it's gotten worse. Sitting in the NBA replay center in Secaucus, New Jersey, I get a hunch about that. After I see all these people on all these TV screens jumping around and hollering at refs, the ruckus appears to be confined to Cleveland. But in here, it feels like the entire universe is disturbed. There's a reason for this. LeBron James is upset.
<v Michael Lewis>当然变好了。怎么可能不呢？谜团是为什么球星、教练和球迷表现得好像它变差了。坐在新泽西州锡考克斯的NBA回放中心，我对这一点有了预感。在我看到所有这些在电视屏幕上蹦蹦跳跳、对着裁判大喊大叫的人之后，喧闹似乎仅限于克利夫兰。但在里面，感觉整个宇宙都被扰动了。这有一个原因。勒布朗·詹姆斯生气了。

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:59.470
<v Michael Lewis>See? He's arguing. There he goes. LeBron James is going from ref to ref. He seems to know which refs to argue with.
<v Michael Lewis>看？他在争论。他去了。勒布朗·詹姆斯正在一个裁判接一个裁判地吵。他似乎知道该和哪些裁判争论。

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:10.260
<v Joe Borgia>Yeah, they're talking about goaltending. So they're talking about it, they're talking about. I think they might change. So James is-- James and the other guy had the best angle.
<v Joe Borgia>是的，他们在谈论干扰球。所以他们在谈论它，他们谈论着。我想他们可能会改变判罚。所以詹姆斯是——詹姆斯和另一个人有最好的视角。

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:15.140
<v Michael Lewis>Do you think that LeBron James has any effect doing that?
<v Michael Lewis>你认为勒布朗·詹姆斯这样做有任何影响吗？

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:16.630
<v Joe Borgia>Um--
<v Joe Borgia>嗯——

00:37:16.630 --> 00:37:34.480
<v Michael Lewis>The ref in Cleveland is not twirling his finger. There's no signal to us to do anything in the Replay Center. LeBron's drama, strictly speaking, is pointless. It's strange the way these players argue. They must think that if they make life unpleasant enough for the ref, he'll think twice before the next call.
<v Michael Lewis>克利夫兰的裁判没有转动手指。回放中心没有信号让我们做任何事。勒布朗的戏剧，严格来说，毫无意义。这些球员争论的方式很奇怪。他们一定认为如果他们让裁判的生活足够不愉快，他在下一次判罚前会三思而后行。

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:35.310
<v Michael Lewis>They didn't review it.
<v Michael Lewis>他们没有回放。

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:36.800
<v Joe Borgia>Well, it's not reviewable.
<v Joe Borgia>嗯，这是不可回放的。

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:37.960
<v Michael Lewis>Goaltending is not reviewable?
<v Michael Lewis>干扰球不可回放？

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:39.830
<v Joe Borgia>Only in the last two minutes. Come on, Michael. Get a--
<v Joe Borgia>只有在最后两分钟。来吧，迈克尔。搞清楚——

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:40.670
<v Michael Lewis>Sorry.
<v Michael Lewis>抱歉。

00:37:40.670 --> 00:38:09.540
<v Ira Glass>It's then that it occurs to me, just looking around the room at 110 TV screens, I've had a hard time following the games, never mind the scores. I sometimes don't even know which teams are playing. But every time a player gets up into a referee's face, I've recognized the player. And I actually don't know that many NBA players, but I know all the ones who pitch these hissy fits, because the only players getting up into the faces of the refs are the famous players, or the coaches who protect them.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时我意识到了，环顾四周110个电视屏幕，我很难跟着比赛节奏，更别说比分了。我有时甚至不知道是哪些球队在打。但每次有球员冲到裁判面前时，我都能认出那个球员。而我其实并不认识那么多NBA球员，但我知道所有那些大吵大闹的球员，因为只有那些著名的球员，或者保护他们的教练才会冲到裁判面前。

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:16.970
<v Ira Glass>Ramona Shelburne put her finger on it. The more aggressive behavior towards refs isn't coming from every player. It's coming from the stars.
<v Ira Glass>拉莫娜·谢尔本指出了这一点。对裁判更具侵略性的行为并不是来自所有球员。它来自球星。

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:33.310
<v Ramona Shelburne>It's just different than in the past. Like, you know, when you're a star, I think there's this feeling that you've sort of risen above reproach. You get used to a certain level of treatment. You get used to people who treat you that way. And then you feel like you should be treated that way.
<v Ramona Shelburne>这和过去不同。就像，你知道，当你是一个球星时，我认为有一种感觉，你已经超脱了批评。你习惯了某种程度的待遇。你习惯了人们那样对待你。然后你觉得你应该得到那种对待。

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:36.810
<v Michael Lewis>Yeah. So a referee-- a referee to that person comes as a shock.
<v Michael Lewis>是的。所以裁判——对于那个人来说，裁判就是一个冲击。

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:50.460
<v Ramona Shelburne>Oh, yeah. You know, with any star and any celebrity, there comes a level of entitlement. So anybody who goes against that, who doesn't treat you like a star, gets put into that hater category.
<v Ramona Shelburne>噢，是的。你知道，对于任何球星和任何名人来说，都伴随着一种权利感。所以任何与此对立、不把你当球星看的人，都会被归入仇恨者类别。

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:59.970
<v Michael Lewis>You want to understand the way the refs are treated? Stop thinking of them as people just doing their best to try to make a game fair, and start thinking of them as haters.
<v Michael Lewis>你想理解裁判被对待的方式吗？停止把他们看作是尽力让比赛公平的人，把他们看作是仇恨者。

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:09.870
<v Dacher Keltner>So we just got really interested in a very simple question of, does this sense of being privileged make you disobey the rules of the road or the laws of the land.
<v Dacher Keltner>所以我们只是对一个非常简单的问题产生了浓厚的兴趣，即，这种被优待的感觉是否会让你违反道路规则或国家法律。

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:18.260
<v Michael Lewis>That's Dacher Keltner. He's a professor of psychology at Cal Berkeley, and someone who wonders about the effect inequality has on people's behavior.
<v Michael Lewis>那是达切尔·克特纳。他是加州大学伯克利分校的心理学教授，也是一个对不平等如何影响人们行为感到好奇的人。

00:39:18.260 --> 00:39:27.720
<v Dacher Keltner>An experience I had at Berkeley where I was riding my bike up this hill. And I got to the four-way stop sign, and I was halfway through this four-way stop sign--
<v Dacher Keltner>我在伯克利时的一次经历，我骑自行车上坡。我到了四向停车标志，我当时已经过了一半——

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:31.040
<v Dacher Keltner>[MOTOR RUNNING]
<v Dacher Keltner>[发动机运转声]

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:54.320
<v Dacher Keltner>And this guy in a black Mercedes rolls through the stop sign, is halfway there, is a foot away from me about to take me out, and he's on his cell phone. And I looked at him. I was ready to take him on, like, all right, buddy, this is it. And what was most telling about this whole experience was he looked at me as if I was in the wrong and I should get out of his way, you know, even though I had made it through the stop sign first.
<v Dacher Keltner>那个开黑色奔驰的家伙滚过了停车标志，走了一半，离我一英尺远，差点撞到我，而他还在打手机。我看着他。我准备和他对质，就像，好吧，伙计，就是这样。而这次经历中最说明问题的是，他看着我就好像我是错的一方，我应该给他让路，你知道，尽管我是第一个通过停车标志的人。

00:39:54.320 --> 00:40:17.550
<v Michael Lewis>So Dacher and a colleague dreamed up this weird experiment. They hid two Berkeley undergraduates in the bushes near four-way stop signs. The undergrads noted the makes of all cars coming through the intersection, assigned them numbers, one to five, according to their market value. A new Mercedes was a five, a Honda was a three, and an old Pacer was a one.
<v Michael Lewis>所以达切尔和一位同事构想了这个奇怪的实验。他们把两名伯克利大学生藏在四向停车标志附近的灌木丛中。学生们记录了所有经过交叉路口的汽车品牌，根据它们的市场价值为它们分配数字，从一到五。新奔驰是5，本田是3，一辆旧帕瑟是1。

00:40:17.550 --> 00:40:31.020
<v Dacher Keltner>We positioned a Berkeley undergrad by a pedestrian zone, and we make sure they look like they want to cross the street. And they're sort of leaning into their pedestrian zone, where it's required by California law to stop.
<v Dacher Keltner>我们将一名伯克利大学生安置在步行区，确保他们看起来想穿过街道。他们有点倾向于步行区，加州法律要求那里停车。

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:36.800
<v Michael Lewis>It's a game of one-on-one at a California crosswalk-- one car versus one pedestrian.
<v Michael Lewis>这是一场加州人行横道上的单挑比赛——一辆车对阵一个行人。

00:40:36.800 --> 00:41:01.160
<v Dacher Keltner>And 0% of the drivers of poor cars zoomed through the pedestrian zone. They all stopped. And 40-some odd percent-- 45% of the drivers of the fives, the rich cars, blazed through the pedestrian zone, and just say, the rules don't apply to me. I'll carry on.
<v Dacher Keltner>而0%的低端车司机冲过了步行区。他们都停下了。而40多个百分点——45%的5分车，即豪车司机，呼啸着冲过步行区，只是说，规则不适用于我。我会继续走。

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:06.840
<v Michael Lewis>This one study led to a bunch of others that showed basically the same pattern of human behavior.
<v Michael Lewis>这项研究引导出了一系列其他研究，基本显示了同样的人类行为模式。

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:20.210
<v Dacher Keltner>Another experiment, we bring people to the lab. And as they're leaving, there's this big bowl of candy. And it's like-- and it says on it, "for the children of the Institute of Human Development," on the bowl.
<v Dacher Keltner>另一个实验，我们把人带到实验室。当他们离开时，有一个大碗糖果。上面写着，“为了人类发展研究所的孩子们”。

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:31.430
<v Dacher Keltner>And we say, oh, you know, take a candy or two as you're leaving. And we count up how many candies they take after they leave the experiment. Privileged people grab a big handful of candy, compared to poorer people.
<v Dacher Keltner>我们说，噢，你知道，离开时拿一两块糖果。我们数一下他们离开实验后拿了多少块。优待的人比起穷人会抓一大把糖果。

00:41:31.430 --> 00:41:34.550
<v Michael Lewis>So let's turn the conversation to something much more important, which is basketball.
<v Michael Lewis>那么让我们把对话转向更重要的事情，也就是篮球。

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:36.410
<v Dacher Keltner>Most important of all.
<v Dacher Keltner>最重要的事情。

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:52.350
<v Michael Lewis>In the last five or six years, the NBA has embarked on essentially a dramatic reform of refereeing. At the same time, the friction between the players, and some of the owners, and some of the coaches, and the refs, is going through the roof.
<v Michael Lewis>在过去五六年里，NBA实质上启动了一场戏剧性的裁判改革。与此同时，球员、部分老板、教练和裁判之间的摩擦正在冲上云霄。

00:41:52.350 --> 00:41:52.550
<v Dacher Keltner>Wow.
<v Dacher Keltner>哇。

00:41:52.550 --> 00:42:08.470
<v Michael Lewis>The source of the outrage is the star players. The people who are getting thrown out of games are Kevin Durant and Steph Curry and James Harden. And the Warriors, the most famous team ever to walk on the court, are the chief culprits-- exhibit A in bad behavior towards refs. So you've got this weird combination.
<v Michael Lewis>愤怒的源头是球星球员。被踢出比赛的人是凯文·杜兰特、斯蒂芬·库里和詹姆斯·哈登。而勇士队，有史以来最著名的球队，是主要罪魁祸首——对待裁判行为不端的例证A。所以你得到了这个奇怪的组合。

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:20.000
<v Dacher Keltner>Yeah. No, that's fascinating. I still remember being a Lakers fan-- you know, the great Magic Johnson teams. And watching Larry Bird do his nine-step lay-up. And I'm like, come on, make the call.
<v Dacher Keltner>是的。不，这太迷人了。我仍然记得自己是个湖人球迷——你知道，伟大的魔术师约翰逊的球队。看着拉里·伯德做他的九步上篮。我想，来吧，判罚啊。

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:32.260
<v Michael Lewis>For most players of his era, that would have counted as traveling. But Larry Bird was like LeBron, the new Mercedes of his day. He played with certain assumptions about the rules and how they applied to him.
<v Michael Lewis>对于他那个时代的多数球员来说，那会被算作走步。但拉里·伯德就像勒布朗，他那个时代的奔驰。他带着关于规则以及它们如何适用于他的特定假设进行比赛。

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.370
<v Michael Lewis>The inequality on a basketball court--
<v Michael Lewis>篮球场上的不平等——

00:42:34.370 --> 00:42:34.770
<v Dacher Keltner>Is profound.
<v Dacher Keltner>是深刻的。

00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:53.630
<v Michael Lewis>--is profound. And it's more profound than it was in Larry Bird's era. Yeah Larry Bird was a millionaire. LeBron James might be a billionaire. And these guys are global franchises. So you got, in a funny way, a microcosm. It's an odd microcosm on a basketball court, of what's going on in the larger society.
<v Michael Lewis>——是深刻的。比拉里·伯德时代更深刻。是的，拉里·伯德是百万富翁。勒布朗·詹姆斯可能是亿万富翁。这些人是全球性的品牌。所以你以一种有趣的方式拥有了一个微观世界。篮球场上是一个奇怪的微观世界，反映了更广泛社会中发生的事情。

00:42:53.630 --> 00:43:06.530
<v Ira Glass>The NBA has set out to ref the games more objectively, more accurately, more fairly. This has enraged the stars and their fans and coaches. You want to know why? Here's what I think.
<v Ira Glass>NBA已经着手更客观、更准确、更公平地执裁比赛。这激怒了球星、他们的球迷和教练。你想知道为什么吗？这就是我的想法。

00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:29.100
<v Ira Glass>The stars used to get more calls in their favor than they do now, just because they were stars. Objective refs have eliminated some of their privilege. The more objectivity there is, the less power they have. And to them, that's outrageous.
<v Ira Glass>球星过去在判罚中获得的好处比现在多，仅仅因为他们是球星。客观的裁判消除了一些特权。客观性越多，他们的权力就越小。对他们来说，这太令人愤慨了。

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:54.010
<v Ira Glass>I think American life just now has at least one thing in common with basketball-- the authority of its referees is under attack. If people don't trust the refs, one day you wake up in a world that seems not just unfair but actually sort of rigged-- that is, it's incapable of becoming fair because the people who benefit from the unfairness have the power to preserve it. Boom.
<v Ira Glass>我想美国生活现在至少有一点和篮球共同之处——裁判的权威正在受到攻击。如果人们不信任裁判，总有一天你会醒来，发现世界不仅看起来不公平，而且实际上被操纵了——也就是说，它无法变得公平，因为受益于不公平的人有权力保护它。轰。

00:43:54.010 --> 00:43:58.020
<v Michael Lewis>Do you flip a switch, and 110 screens go dark?
<v Michael Lewis>你拨动一个开关，110个屏幕就变黑了吗？

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:02.980
<v Joe Borgia>All the little small screens you gotta do manually. The big TVs we got the remote for.
<v Joe Borgia>所有那些小屏幕你得手动操作。大电视我们有遥控器。

00:44:02.980 --> 00:44:16.560
<v Michael Lewis>Most nights, Joe Borgia stays at the Replay Center until almost 2:00 in the morning-- just him and a couple of refs staring at tiny slivers of basketball games, trying to impose justice on powerful people who don't want it.
<v Michael Lewis>大多数夜晚，乔·博尔吉亚留在锡考克斯的回放中心直到凌晨2点——只有他和几个裁判盯着篮球比赛的微小碎片，试图强行将正义施加给那些不想要它的强大人物。

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:22.060
<v Joe Borgia>They think I'm nuts. I am nuts. That's another story. All refs are nuts. You gotta be.
<v Joe Borgia>他们认为我疯了。我是疯了。那是另一个故事。所有裁判都疯了。你必须是。

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:22.960
<v Michael Lewis>You do?
<v Michael Lewis>你必须？

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:26.500
<v Joe Borgia>You have to be partially off. It's a 100% negative business.
<v Joe Borgia>你必须部分脱节。这是一个100%负面的行业。

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:29.120
<v Michael Lewis>100% negative business.
<v Michael Lewis>100%负面的行业。

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:32.500
<v Joe Borgia>That's why my son doesn't want to ref. Dad, I don't like people yelling at me.
<v Joe Borgia>这就是为什么我儿子不想当裁判。爸爸，我不喜欢人们对我大喊大叫。

00:44:32.500 --> 00:44:50.550
<v Michael Lewis>One day a young Borgia naturally becomes a referee. The next, he doesn't. One day most people think the refs are more or less fair-- or at any rate, they don't spend a lot of time blaming them for all their problems. The next day, they wake up to radical inequality.
<v Michael Lewis>有一天年轻的博尔吉亚自然成为了一名裁判。第二天，他不再是了。有一天大多数人认为裁判或多或少是公平的——或者至少，他们不花时间把所有问题归咎于他们。第二天，他们醒来面对极端的不平等。

00:44:50.550 --> 00:45:05.920
<v Michael Lewis>The people on top, the elites, think they're special. They behave as people do when they think they're special. Young people emulate them, without even thinking about it. They just assume that's how you act if you're a star or want to be.
<v Michael Lewis>顶层的人，精英们，认为自己很特别。当人们认为自己特别时，他们的行为也会随之改变。年轻人甚至在不假思索地模仿他们。他们只是假设如果你是一个球星或想成为球星，你就得那样表现。

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:16.050
<v Michael Lewis>My first question is-- why, when you hit a three point shot, which you often do, why in the past have you pounded your chest and pointed to the sky?
<v Michael Lewis>我的第一个问题是——为什么当你投中三分球时，你经常这样做，为什么过去你拍拍胸口并指向天空？

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:19.920
<v Walker>I did it because the people on the NBA-- in the NBA did it.
<v Walker>我这样做是因为NBA的人——在NBA里的人这样做。

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:22.880
<v Michael Lewis>What do you think they're doing? What does it mean?
<v Michael Lewis>你认为他们在做什么？那意味着什么？

00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:25.900
<v Walker>I don't know. Just, like, I'm cool.
<v Walker>我不知道。只是，像，我很酷。

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:27.910
<v Michael Lewis>Do you believe in God?
<v Michael Lewis>你相信上帝吗？

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:32.490
<v Walker>No. I knew-- I mean, now I knew what it meant. But no, I don't.
<v Walker>不。我当时知道——我的意思是，现在我知道它的含义了。但没有，我不相信。

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.950
<v Michael Lewis>So what does it mean?
<v Michael Lewis>那么那意味着什么？

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:37.200
<v Walker>Basically, it's like, thank you, God.
<v Walker>基本上，它就像，谢谢你，上帝。

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:43.560
<v Michael Lewis>For hitting a three point shot? Do you think God was responsible when Klay Thompson hit a three point shot?
<v Michael Lewis>为了投进一个三分球？你认为当克莱·汤普森投进三分球时，是上帝的功劳吗？

00:45:43.560 --> 00:45:57.860
<v Walker>To be honest, if God was watching over everybody whenever they hit a three point shot, I don't think that He would be able to, like, actually make them make the shot.
<v Walker>老实说，如果上帝在每个人投进三分球时都照看着，我不认为他能够，像，真的让他们投中那一球。

00:45:57.860 --> 00:46:03.780
<v Michael Lewis>So do you have anything you'd like to say to the referees of the world before we turn this recording off?
<v Michael Lewis>所以你有什么想对世界裁判说的吗，在我们关掉录音之前？

00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:07.230
<v Walker>Don't pick sides, unless it's my side.
<v Walker>别站队，除非是我的这一边。

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:14.070
<v Michael Lewis>Thank you.
<v Michael Lewis>谢谢。

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:48.970
<v Ira Glass>Michael Lewis. His new podcast, Against The Rules, covers all kinds of institutions in America where people do not trust the refs anymore. It is made by Pushkin Industries. Find it wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>迈克尔·刘易斯。他的新播客《规则之外》，涵盖了美国各种机构，人们不再信任那里的裁判。它由普希金工业公司制作。在你获取播客的地方都可以找到。

00:46:48.970 --> 00:47:03.710
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a teenager grapples with the document that's supposed to enforce fairness above all others. I'm talking about the United States Constitution. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一位青少年努力解读被认为旨在高于一切强制公平的文件。我说的是美国宪法。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:26.940
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "No Fair," stories about people young and old all over our country feeling a sense of injustice, feeling wronged, and not getting satisfaction from the people in charge. We have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The Fairer Sex. And for this act, let's go to Broadway. There's a show right now that starts with this true story.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，“不公平”，讲述了全国各地年轻人和老年人感受到不公正、感到受冤枉，并无法从负责人那里得到满意答复的故事。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，性别更公平。而对于这一幕，让我们去百老汇。现在有一部戏是以这个真实故事开头的。

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:51.820
<v Heidi Schreck>When I was 15 years old, I would travel the country giving speeches about the Constitution at American Legion halls for prize money. This was a scheme invented by my mom to help me pay for college. I would travel to big cities like Denver and Fresno. I would give a speech, win a whole bunch of money, and then bring it back to put in my little safety deposit box for later.
<v Heidi Schreck>当我15岁时，我会走遍全国在美国军团大厅演讲宪法，以获得奖金。这是我妈妈发明的一个方案，用来帮助我支付大学学费。我会去丹佛和弗雷斯诺这样的大城市。我会发表演讲，赢一大笔钱，然后带回来放进我的小保险箱留作后用。

00:47:51.820 --> 00:48:06.040
<v Ira Glass>This is Heidi Schreck in the show What the Constitution Means to Me. I loved this show. It's totally original in what it's about and in how it tells its story. It's very funny. It's a very patriotic show in this totally uncorny way.
<v Ira Glass>这是海蒂·舒瑞克在《宪法对我意味着什么》这部剧中的表现。我非常喜欢这部剧。它在主题和讲述故事的方式上完全原创。它非常有趣。这是一部以完全不俗气的方式呈现的非常爱国的演出。

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:28.320
<v Ira Glass>In the show, Heidi Schreck recreates the idealistic speech that she gave at 15 that won her enough money to pay for her whole college education. And then she talks about the Constitution and about her own life. Our show today is about fairness, and Heidi Schreck, to be clear, says that her 15-year-old self definitely saw the Constitution as a powerful instrument enforcing fairness.
<v Ira Glass>在剧中，海蒂·舒瑞克重现了她15岁时发表的理想主义演讲，那次演讲为她赢得了足以支付全部大学教育的钱。然后她谈论了宪法和她自己的生活。我们今天的节目是关于公平的，海蒂·舒瑞克，明确地说，她15岁的自己绝对把宪法视为强制公平的强大工具。

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:36.500
<v Heidi Schreck>I believed it made our country fairer, more equal, more democratic. I believed that wholeheartedly.
<v Heidi Schreck>我相信它让我们的国家更公平、更平等、更民主。我全心全意地相信这一点。

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:37.190
<v Ira Glass>At 15?
<v Ira Glass>15岁时？

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:37.980
<v Heidi Schreck>At 15.
<v Heidi Schreck>15岁时。

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:41.890
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm. You said that as a kid you had a crush on the Constitution.
<v Ira Glass>嗯嗯。你说你小时候对宪法情有独钟。

00:48:41.890 --> 00:48:43.240
<v Heidi Schreck>(CHUCKLING) Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>（笑声）是的。

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:45.330
<v Ira Glass>What does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>那意味着什么？

00:48:45.330 --> 00:49:24.510
<v Heidi Schreck>I was really excited by it. I was a very idealistic, nerdy young person. And researching and reading about the Constitution-- my dad is a history teacher and talked very passionately about the genius of this document and its ability to evolve and to somehow surpass and rectify the flaws of its founders. Like, this idea that there was a document that could be created by flawed people that could be better than the people who made it.
<v Heidi Schreck>我对此感到非常兴奋。我是一个非常理想主义、书呆子气的年轻人。研究和阅读关于宪法的内容——我爸爸是一位历史老师，他非常热情地谈论这份文件的天才之处，以及它演化并以某种方式超越和纠正其创始人缺陷的能力。就像，有一种想法，即存在一份由有缺陷的人创造的文件，它可能比创造它的人更好。

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:35.840
<v Ira Glass>When you say that I'm reminded of this famous quote from Bill Clinton where he said-- and I think this is such a beautiful quote, too-- where he said, there's nothing that's wrong with America that what's right with America can't fix.
<v Ira Glass>当你这么说时，我被比尔·克林顿那句名言提醒了——我认为那也是一句非常美丽的名言——他说，美国没有任何问题是美国正确的东西不能解决的。

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:55.410
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes. I mean, Frederick Douglass has the similar quote which is that, like, yes, the Constitution was founded on the great evil compromise of slavery but that it also contained the means by which we could rectify that evil.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。我的意思是，弗雷德里克·道格拉斯也有类似的引言，就是，是的，宪法是建立在奴隶制这个伟大的邪恶妥协之上的，但它也包含了我们可以用来纠正那种邪恶的手段。

00:49:55.410 --> 00:50:00.420
<v Ira Glass>And so your play is partly about that hopeful feeling that you had about the Constitution when you were a kid.
<v Ira Glass>所以你的剧部分是关于你小时候对宪法的那种希望感。

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:00.680
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:17.810
<v Ira Glass>But also how you came to see it once you became an adult, as a deeply flawed document. You talk in the play about how especially the Constitution and American law really did not address things that affect you and members of your family, especially the women in your family, for just a long time.
<v Ira Glass>但也关于你成年后如何看待它，作为一份有严重缺陷的文件。你在戏中谈到，尤其是宪法和美国法律长期以来确实没有解决影响你和你家人成员的事情，尤其是你家里的女性。

00:50:17.810 --> 00:50:18.550
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes, yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的，是的。

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:26.490
<v Ira Glass>And when it did finally get around to addressing women, it was late, and it came to it kind of creakily.
<v Ira Glass>而当它最终开始关注女性时，已经很晚了，而且它显得有点吱嘎作响。

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:27.680
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes, very creakily.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的，非常吱嘎作响。

00:50:27.680 --> 00:50:40.050
<v Ira Glass>And there's a large section of the play where you talk about how to get women to be covered by the Constitution, lawyers and the justices had to kind of jury rig women into having rights using, for example, the Ninth Amendment.
<v Ira Glass>剧中有一个很大的章节谈到，为了让女性被宪法覆盖，律师和大法官不得不通过使用，例如，第九修正案，把女性强行塞进去拥有权利。

00:50:40.050 --> 00:50:40.470
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:50:40.470 --> 00:50:43.720
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you to just explain what the Ninth Amendment does?
<v Ira Glass>我可以请你解释一下第九修正案的作用吗？

00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:57.120
<v Heidi Schreck>Sure, sure. So the Ninth Amendment, which was my favorite as a kid, says, the enumeration in the Constitution of certain rights shall not be construed to deny or disparage other rights.
<v Heidi Schreck>当然，当然。所以第九修正案，我小时候最喜欢的一条，说，宪法中对某些权利的列举，不得被解释为否认或贬低其他权利。

00:50:57.120 --> 00:51:00.480
<v Ira Glass>I just want to say for radio, anyone that's listening, that you're just reciting that from memory.
<v Ira Glass>我只想为广播说一句，对于任何听众，你是在背诵它。

00:51:00.480 --> 00:51:02.040
<v Heidi Schreck>I'm just reciting from memory. Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>我只是在凭记忆背诵。是的。

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:03.270
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:28.120
<v Heidi Schreck>Well, it's technically "shall not be construed to deny or disparage others retained by the people," meaning that just because a certain right is not explicitly given to you in the Constitution, it doesn't mean you don't have that right. This amendment allows for the fact that the founders could not have imagined the future that we're living in now.
<v Heidi Schreck>嗯，技术上是“不得被解释为否认或贬低由人民保留的其他权利”，意思是仅仅因为某项权利没有明确列在宪法中，并不意味着你没有那项权利。这条修正案允许存在这样一种事实，即创始人无法想象我们现在生活的未来。

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:32.410
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's like them admitting it. It's like, we can't think through every possible thing in this one document.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，这就像他们承认这一点。就像，我们不能在这单一的文件中考虑到每一种可能的情况。

00:51:32.410 --> 00:51:38.370
<v Heidi Schreck>Right. I also like to think that in 300 years they knew that the world would look very different.
<v Heidi Schreck>对。我也喜欢认为在300年后，他们知道世界看起来会非常不同。

00:51:38.370 --> 00:51:50.110
<v Ira Glass>And in the play, though, you say, to actually get this thing to apply to women, Justice William O. Douglas has to come up with a concept which is so strange and so almost poetic, this idea of the penumbra.
<v Ira Glass>但在剧中，你说，为了真正让这东西适用于女性，威廉·O·道格拉斯大法官必须想出一个概念，它是如此陌生，以至于几乎是诗意的，这个半影的概念。

00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:51.640
<v Heidi Schreck>Right.
<v Heidi Schreck>对。

00:51:51.640 --> 00:52:11.800
<v Heidi Schreck>What is a penumbra? Here I am standing in the light, and there you are sitting in the darkness. And this space between us, this space right here of partial illumination, this shadowy space right here, this is a penumbra.
<v Heidi Schreck>什么是半影？我就站在这里的光亮中，而你坐在黑暗中。而在我们之间的这个空间，就在这里的这种部分照明空间，这个阴影空间，就是一个半影。

00:52:11.800 --> 00:52:35.560
<v Heidi Schreck>William O. Douglas loved this penumbra metaphor and said that you could therefore take this idea that somewhere in the shadows of the Constitution, in this kind of in between shadowy space, are our rights we have not yet named. You could use the Ninth Amendment to essentially, like, shine a little light into the dark corners and say, OK, over there.
<v Heidi Schreck>威廉·O·道格拉斯喜欢这个半影隐喻，并说你可以因此采取这种观点，即在宪法的阴影中，在中间这种阴影空间里，藏着我们尚未命名的权利。你可以利用第九修正案，基本上就像，照亮黑暗角落，说，好的，在那边。

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:41.800
<v Ira Glass>And then as you say in the show, the Ninth Amendment combines with the 14th Amendment in a kind of Wonder Twins, power activation moment--
<v Ira Glass>正如你在节目中所说，第九修正案与第14修正案结合，在一种奇迹双胞胎激活权力的瞬间——

00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:42.160
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:52:42.160 --> 00:52:43.260
<v Ira Glass>--to decide Roe versus Wade.
<v Ira Glass>——来裁定罗伊诉韦德案。

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:43.930
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:52:43.930 --> 00:52:53.460
<v Ira Glass>But the Ninth Amendment is also the way that birth control gets legalized in the '60s. And let's hear-- we're going to hear an excerpt now from the play.
<v Ira Glass>但第九修正案也是避孕在60年代合法化的方式。让我们听听——我们要播放一段剧中的摘录。

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:53.890
<v Heidi Schreck>OK.
<v Heidi Schreck>好的。

00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:59.580
<v Ira Glass>And this is where you talk about how that came about. And it begins with you explaining how you and a friend got birth control.
<v Ira Glass>这是你谈论它是如何发生的。它开始于你解释你和一个朋友如何获得节育措施。

00:52:59.580 --> 00:53:00.450
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:53:00.450 --> 00:53:04.620
<v Ira Glass>The same year you were doing these contests, when you were 15 you went to Planned Parenthood.
<v Ira Glass>同一年你在参加比赛，当你15岁时去了计划生育协会。

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:14.890
<v Heidi Schreck>Neither of us were having sex yet, but just we wanted to be on birth control just in case. You know, just in case we were in a hot tub and sperms might happen to attack us.
<v Heidi Schreck>我们两人都还没发生性行为，但只是我们想为了以防万一而进行节育。你知道，以防万一我们在热水浴缸里，精子可能会攻击我们。

00:53:14.890 --> 00:53:18.890
<v Heidi Schreck>[LAUGHTER]
<v Heidi Schreck>[笑声]

00:53:18.890 --> 00:53:41.540
<v Heidi Schreck>OK, I mean, you can laugh, but be careful. Or, you know, in case of a real attack. What I didn't realize at the time is that birth control had only been legal for all women in this country for about 15 years. I mean, I was 15. So I thought it had been legal since the dawn of time. But no, no.
<v Heidi Schreck>好的，我的意思是，你可以笑，但要小心。或者，你知道，以防真正的袭击。我当时没意识到的是，节育在那之前在全国范围内对所有女性合法化才大约15年。我的意思是，我当时15岁。所以我以为它从时间之初就合法了。但没有，没有。

00:53:41.540 --> 00:54:16.100
<v Heidi Schreck>In 1965, this incredible woman, Estelle Griswold, got herself arrested for dispensing IUDs to poor women at her Connecticut Planned Parenthood. She faced a year in prison, took her case all the way to the Supreme Court, and this, this is actually when William O. Douglas first pulled out his big penumbra metaphor. This is when he said for the first time that one thing the Constitution surely guarantees is the right to privacy, and that this allows a woman to put in an IUD as long as she is married and as long as her husband says that it is OK.
<v Heidi Schreck>1965年，这位了不起的女士，埃斯特尔·格里斯沃尔德，因在她的康涅狄格州计划生育协会向贫困妇女发放宫内节育器而被捕。她面临一年监禁，将案件一直告到了最高法院，而这，实际上是威廉·O·道格拉斯第一次拿出他的大半影隐喻。这是他第一次说宪法肯定保证的一件事就是隐私权，而这允许女性只要结婚并且丈夫同意，就可以放入宫内节育器。

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:18.560
<v Heidi Schreck>[LAUGHTER]
<v Heidi Schreck>[笑声]

00:54:18.560 --> 00:54:39.320
<v Heidi Schreck>This is a very scary moment for William O. Douglas because nobody understands the Ninth Amendment-- nobody, except for me at 15. Justice Scalia said he didn't even remember studying it in law school. But they had to dig up this amendment that nobody understands, because there was just no other way to deal with the female body.
<v Heidi Schreck>这对威廉·O·道格拉斯来说是一个非常可怕的时刻，因为没有人理解第九修正案——没有人，除了我15岁的时候。斯卡利亚大法官说他甚至不记得在法学院学习过它。但他们必须挖掘出这条没有人理解的修正案，因为除了它，根本没有别的方法来处理女性身体。

00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:56.390
<v Heidi Schreck>Because they all wanted to make birth control legal. They did. They wanted it because-- well, because I found out that William O. Douglas, who was my hero when I was 15, Justice William O. Douglas was 67, and he was having an affair with a 22-year-old college student.
<v Heidi Schreck>因为他们都想让节育合法化。他们确实想。他们想要它是因为——嗯，因为我发现威廉·O·道格拉斯，我15岁时的英雄，威廉·O·道格拉斯大法官当时67岁，他和一个22岁的大学生有染。

00:54:56.390 --> 00:54:56.900
<v Heidi Schreck>[GASPING]
<v Heidi Schreck>[喘气声]

00:54:56.900 --> 00:55:24.490
<v Heidi Schreck>So I'm thinking-- right? They want to find a way to get the birth control flowing. Actually, Arabella, can you play a snippet of the Supreme Court recording? This is the actual argument. So remember, it's 1965. There will not be a woman on this court until Sandra Day O'Connor arrives in 1981. Here are nine men deciding the fate of birth control.
<v Heidi Schreck>所以我当时在想——对吧？他们想找到一种方法让节育普及。实际上，阿拉贝拉，你能播放一段最高法院录音的片段吗？这是实际的争论。记住，是1965年。在1981年桑德拉·戴·奥康纳到达之前，这个法院不会有女性。这是九名男子决定节育的命运。

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:35.950
<v Supreme Court Justice>All of these devices that are covered, that each of them has the potential dual function of acting in a contraceptive capacity and as a prevention of disease?
<v Supreme Court Justice>所有这些被覆盖的设备，它们每一个都具有作为避孕和预防疾病的双重功能？

00:55:35.950 --> 00:55:42.850
<v Supreme Court Justice>It's probably only true with respect to some. But some get by under the term "feminine hygiene."
<v Supreme Court Justice>这可能只适用于其中一部分。但有些以“女性卫生”的名义混过去了。

00:55:42.850 --> 00:56:02.570
<v Supreme Court Justice>And all this-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- uh-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- uh-- I just don't know about. But-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- uh-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- they are-- they are all sold in Connecticut drugstores on one theory or another.
<v Supreme Court Justice>而所有这些——[清嗓子]——呃——[清嗓子]——呃——我只是不知道关于这个。但——[清嗓子]——呃——[清嗓子]——它们——它们都在康涅狄格州的药店里以某种理论出售。

00:56:02.570 --> 00:56:16.190
<v Supreme Court Justice>Is there anything on the record to-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- to indicate-- [CLEARING THROAT]-- the standard of birth rate in Connecticut vis-a-vis the states that don't have such laws?
<v Supreme Court Justice>记录上有什么——[清嗓子]——指示——[清嗓子]——康涅狄格州的出生率标准与没有这种法律的州相比吗？

00:56:16.190 --> 00:56:18.780
<v Heidi Schreck>It's, like, four hours of that.
<v Heidi Schreck>差不多有四个小时都是那样。

00:56:18.780 --> 00:56:20.430
<v Heidi Schreck>[LAUGHTER]
<v Heidi Schreck>[笑声]

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:45.310
<v Ira Glass>When Heidi Schreck was publicizing her show, she did this interview with Tony Kushner, the playwright and the Pulitzer prize winner, probably best known for Angels in America. And in this interview, they had this interesting exchange. Kushner says to her that he thought that her show was partly about this thing that he has been writing about and thinking about ever since he wrote that movie about Abraham Lincoln a few years ago that Stephen Spielberg directed.
<v Ira Glass>当海蒂·舒瑞克为她的节目宣传时，她接受了托尼·库什纳的采访，他是剧作家、普利策奖得主，可能因《天使在美国》而最出名。在这次采访中，他们进行了有趣的交流。库什纳对她说，他认为她的演出部分是关于他自从几年前写了那部史蒂芬·斯皮尔伯格执导的亚伯拉罕·林肯电影后就一直在写和思考的事情。

00:56:45.310 --> 00:57:24.430
<v Ira Glass>Kushner said that in his view, a central question of democracy is that there needs to be some kind of mystical bond holding everybody together. Lincoln talked about the mystic chords of memory and very consciously tried to pull everybody together into allegiance for the Union. But Kushner said that if you actually interrogate what the thing is that is actually pulling us together, it's really just a stack of paper, right? It's just an act of faith that we have with each other. And he pointed out that how, of course, everybody knows right now this act of faith, this feeling that we are all in this together and we are one people who can work things out together, that is in such short supply.
<v Ira Glass>库什纳说，在他看来，民主的一个核心问题是需要某种神秘的纽带将每个人联系在一起。林肯谈到了记忆的神秘和弦，并非常有意识地试图将每个人拉到一起忠诚于联邦。但库什纳说，如果你真正审视那件正在把我们拉在一起的东西，那真的只是一叠纸，对吧？这只是我们彼此之间的一种信念行为。他指出，当然，现在每个人都知道这种信念行为，这种感觉我们都在一起、是一个可以共同解决问题的民族的感觉，是多么稀缺。

00:57:24.430 --> 00:58:02.270
<v Ira Glass>And the very idea of teenagers giving speeches about that stack of papers, about the Constitution, and its importance and its values, Kushner said-- I'm quoting here-- "It feels like an ethos of a not very distant and yet somehow very distant past, where people believe in fairness, that the superstructure that we've all sworn allegiance to, the Constitution, is going to create opportunities and possibilities." That really hit me when I read it. And I asked Heidi Schreck in our interview if she agreed that the America where she gave those speeches when she was 15 seemed very far away.
<v Ira Glass>而青少年发表演讲关于那叠纸、关于宪法及其重要性和价值的整个想法，库什纳说——我引用这里——“这感觉像是一种并不遥远却又显得非常遥远的过去的精神，那时人们相信公平，认为我们都宣誓效忠的超结构，即宪法，将创造机遇和可能性。”当我读到这一段时，我深受震撼。在采访中，我问海蒂·舒瑞克，她是否同意她15岁时发表演讲的那个美国看起来已经非常遥远了。

00:58:02.270 --> 00:58:03.410
<v Heidi Schreck>Yes.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的。

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:04.580
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么讲？

00:58:04.580 --> 00:58:36.780
<v Heidi Schreck>Well, so this was the '80s, between '86 and '89. First of all, I felt-- I mean, I was very liberal in high school. You know, I was feminist, liberal. Like, my speeches reflected those values, and I remember giving a speech about the Second Amendment, which I was arguing for gun control, because there had just been a shooting in California at a McDonald's. And I was arguing for strict gun control laws in front of a room of men who, you know, all owned guns and went hunting. And I won that contest.
<v Heidi Schreck>嗯，那是80年代，在86年到89年之间。首先，我觉得——我的意思是，我在高中时非常自由派。你知道，我是女性主义者、自由主义者。就像，我的演讲反映了那些价值观，我记得我做过关于第二修正案的演讲，我是在争论枪支管制，因为当时在加州的一家麦当劳发生了一起枪击案。我是在一群男人面前争论严格的枪支管制法律，你知道，他们都拥有枪支并去狩猎。而且我赢得了那场比赛。

00:58:36.780 --> 00:59:02.150
<v Heidi Schreck>And I always felt a sense that these men I was, you know, giving the speeches for supported my right to have a different view than they did, supported my right as an American to express myself, believed it was important in a democracy that we have these conversations together. So that felt different to me. I will also say, this was, like, the very beginning of Rush Limbaugh, you know, and then Fox News.
<v Heidi Schreck>而且我一直感觉到这些我为他们做演讲的男人，你知道，支持我有与他们不同的观点的权利，支持我作为美国人表达自己的权利，相信在民主国家中我们共同进行这些对话很重要。所以那对我来说感觉不同。我还要说，这简直就是拉什·林博开始的时候，你知道，然后是福克斯新闻。

00:59:02.150 --> 00:59:05.270
<v Ira Glass>And those men who gave you award money after you argued against the--
<v Ira Glass>而那些在你反对——之后给你奖金的男人们——

00:59:05.270 --> 00:59:06.060
<v Heidi Schreck>Yeah, yeah.
<v Heidi Schreck>是的，是的。

00:59:06.060 --> 00:59:10.880
<v Ira Glass>--the Second Amendment, you think that that would be-- it might be harder today.
<v Ira Glass>——第二修正案，你认为那会是——今天可能更困难。

00:59:10.880 --> 00:59:29.660
<v Heidi Schreck>I think so. Although I will say, I looked on YouTube at other American Legion speeches. And they still seem to be-- like, the winners often seem to be quite liberal. So at least maybe in the space of the American Legion, there's still room for this kind of dialogue.
<v Heidi Schreck>我想是吧。虽然我不得不说，我在YouTube上看了其他美国军团的演讲。而且他们看起来仍然是——就像，获胜者似乎经常是相当自由派的。所以至少在美国军团的空间里，似乎还有这种对话的余地。

00:59:29.660 --> 00:59:30.560
<v Ira Glass>I hope.
<v Ira Glass>我希望如此。

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:38.070
<v Heidi Schreck>I hope too.
<v Heidi Schreck>我也希望如此。

00:59:38.070 --> 01:00:02.850
<v Ira Glass>Heidi Schreck. If you're visiting New York City, her show What the Constitution Means to Me, is running through July on Broadway. A little Constitutional footnote here-- after we recorded that interview, I learned that the 14th and Ninth Amendments did not really combine Wonder Twins style to decide Roe versus Wade. Technically, the 14th decided Roe, with the Ninth justifying an important precedent that's cited in the case.
<v Ira Glass>海蒂·舒瑞克。如果你在访问纽约市，她的节目《宪法对我意味着什么》正在百老汇上演，一直持续到7月。这里有一个小小的宪法脚注——在我们录完那次采访后，我了解到第14和第九修正案并没有真正结合成奇迹双胞胎风格来裁定罗伊诉韦德案。技术上是第14修正案裁定了罗伊案，而第九修正案证明了一个案例中引用的重要先例的正当性。

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:44.390
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THIS LAND IS YOUR LAND" BY SHARON JONES AND THE DAP-KINGS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐——莎朗·琼斯和Dap-Kings演唱的《这片土地是你的土地》]

01:00:44.390 --> 01:01:18.660
